9-year-old boy on the run in Tampere was found
The police have had a search mission due to a 9-year-old boy's run in Tampere on Tuesday evening.
A notice of disappearance against a boy who has left Western Tampere with a friend has spread on social media.
According to the Situation Centre of the police, the boy has been contacted and there is no danger to life and health.
The boy's father told Ilta-Sanomat around midnight that the boy had been found.
She's fine, and now we're going to start the follow-up.
The boy was found near the home, the father said.
Rondo, on the other hand, tore the goal difference into Messi
Cristiano Ronaldo scored five goals in the Spanish league this weekend and also continued to score in the Champions League.
The battle between Lionel Messi and Cristiano Ronaldo from the Champions League goalie turned, at least briefly, to the advantage of the latter.
Real Madrid's Ronaldo moved to the top of an all-time scoring record when he shot two shots from penalty kicks and one score in the net by Shahtar Donetski.
Ronaldo increased his goal balance to 80 when Barcelona's Messi, who was tied with him before Tuesday, has 77 goals.
Real won the game 4-0.
Juventus have stumbled in the Italian Football League in Serie A at the start of the season, but in Group D of the Champions League they started strongly with a 2-1 away win over Manchester City.
The winning goal was scored just over ten minutes before the end by Alvaro Morata of Juventus.
City's offense was blunted by the absence of Sergei Aguero, who suffered a knee problem.
Aguero, who was injured in this weekend's Premier League game, only came on the pitch in the last ten minutes.
Manchester's second club, United, also experienced a toughest in the Netherlands against PSV Endhoven after suffering a 1-2 loss.
ManU's 20-year-old winger Luke Shaw's away game lasted only a quarter of an hour.
PSV's Hector Moreno's dangerous-looking sliding tackle hit the ManU pack, which attacked the goal, so badly that Shaw was likely to fracture his tibia.
Shaw lay on the lawn of Eindhoven's home stadium a dozen minutes before medical personnel left to transport him to the hospital.
Memphis Depay took the visitors into a 1-0 lead in the 41st minute, but ManU's pleasure lasted only a few minutes.
Moreno equalized before the break in extra time.
PSV's winning goal was scored in the second half by Luciano Narsingh.
Goaling in the Champions League was started by Paris Saint-Germain in Group A.
It was Angel Di Maria, who had switched Manchester United's jersey to PSG.
Di Maria broke through from the right and lifted the ball into the back corner past Malmö goalkeeper Johan Wiland.
Edinson Cavani scored 2-0 for PSG in the second half.
PSG has been ranked among the season's winners.
The presumption is not wrong, but Malmö, one of the league's small clubs, isn't the best comparison.
PSG star Zlatan Ibrahimovic faced his professional starting club Malmö FF for the first time.
Ibrahimovic started his career in Malmö in 1999.
Roman Eremenko played 90 minutes when his team, Moscow's TsSKA, lost to Wolfsburg 0-1 in Group B in Germany.
Opposition party immediately demanded resignation - the president gave the wrong speech
Zimbabwe's President Robert Mugabe made the same speech he had made a month ago.
Zimbabwe's President Robert Mugabe has made the same speech he made at the end of last month.
Mugabe didn't seem to realize during his 25-minute speech that the speech was the same.
The speech raised doubts as to whether 91-year-old Mugabe is in full spirit.
Mugabe was urged by the main opposition party MDC to resignate because he's too old to rule.
Mugabe's aides rushed to say that the confusion had only taken place on Mugabe's headquarters and that the president had done nothing wrong.
Mugabe's speech dealt with China's influence on the country's economy.
Germany and Austria want an extraordinary summit on the refugee crisis
Germany and Austria have proposed an extraordinary summit of EU leaders next week due to the refugee crisis.
Donald Tusk, President-in-Chief of the European Council, assessed the request.
German Chancellor Angela Merkel said state leaders should be discussing cooperation with the countries of origin and on how to more effectively register in Europe.
According to Merkel, negotiations on quotas for asylum seekers are part of the home ministers' table.
The teacher admits he shot his girlfriend during an emergency call
Shannon Lamb, a university teacher with just a little quivering, tells the police during the emergency call that he shot his girlfriend and that the police have to come to their house.
Lamb emphasized that her “golden dog” is alive and probably shocked, and said that the contact details of the deceased woman’s next of kin can be found on her phone.
Inside the house, the cops founced a handwritten message from Amy Prentiss’s body and notebook: ‘I’m so sorry I wish I’d been able to take this back and she’s the only woman who’s ever loved me,’ said the letter, which, according to experts, was signed by Lambi.
There was no indication that Lamb, who taught two online courses at Delta State University in Cleveland, Minnesota, had already traveled almost 500 kilometers to the school campus, where the police believe he shot and killed a well-liked history teacher, Ethan Schmidt, at the door of his office.
Lynn Buford, Chief of Police at Delta State University, said the university's officials heard about the shooting at 10:18 a.m.
He said Lamb made a fatal emergency call some time afterwards.
By the end of the day, the number of deaths rose by one more: Lamb committed suicide as the police besieged him.
Authorities were still trying to determine Lamb's motivation a day after pupils and staff had to hide behind locked-doors due to a school shooting.
Investigators on both sides of the state have released reports that students and staff described Lambia as a talented teacher who, however, may have problems.
The students said they waited for his classes.
Police in Gautier's place of death in Prentiss said Lambi didn't have a violent past or a criminal record.
Schmidt himself had included Lamb in a book he wrote, in which he thanked “great people” with whom he shared his academic life.
Both taught at the Department of Social Sciences and History, which has a total of 17 staff members, and many of the pupils participated in the courses of both men.
On the other hand, there were some signs of problems.
A student named Brandon Beavers praised Lambi, but said he also seemed nervous and restless, “as if something had been wrong.”
Mikel Sykes, another student, said Lamb said he was under a lot of stress at the end of 2014-15.
Lamb had previously requested sick leave from Delta State University, saying that he had some kind of health problem.
This year, he only taught two online courses.
Recent changes in the university's remuneration principles meant that although Lamb had worked hard for his doctorate, it did not grant him automatic career development and the position of assistant professor.
William LaForge, the president of the university, said there was no contradiction between Lamb and Schmidt, as far as he knows, but that “there was clearly something in Lambi’s mind.”
Lamb is no longer able to answer these questions.
After Lamb escaped from the campus, the police were able to trace him when he moved back to Mississisippi from the state of Arkansas.
Before he was arrested, Lamb shot himself with a .38-caliber pistol in the backyard of a house about one and a half miles south of his parents' house in Mississippi's Greenville suburban area, Methel Johnson, a medical examiner at Washington County.
Lambi's car was still running in the driveway.
It wasn't immediately clear why Lamb went to this very house, even though Johnson said he believed Lamb knew the people who lived there.
Lamb grew up in the area.
Lamb started working at the university in 2009 and taught geography and education. The university has 3500 students and is located in a city with a population of about 12 000.
Lamb completed a doctoral degree in educational science in the spring.
One of Lambi's old friends described him as intelligent, charismatic and funny.
Carla Hairston said she was 15 years old and Lamb was 20 when they met through mutual friends.
Hairston and his friends were in high school, and Lamb was a funny, older guy who for many years tried to teach Hairston to play guitar.
He was quite a charmer at that time.
“All the girls were molten wax when he was there,” said Hairston, a 40-year-old who lives in Brandon, the suburb of Jackson.
Lamb and Prentiss had apparently been dating for a while.
Lamb told in an emergency call that he killed his wife, but there's no evidence of their marriage.
They had a dog named Lightning, who lived with them in a riverside brick house in Gautier.
Police said the dog was fine.
Prentissi's ex-husband Shawn O'Steen said they divorced 15 years ago, but remained friends. They now have a 19-year-old daughter.
‘He’s really shocked,’ O’Steen described his daughter.
He was his mother's best friend.
O’Steen said he hadn’t met Lamb, but had heard from his daughter Abigail that Lamb was interested in music and played blues.
Lamb met Prentiss when Lamb and Abigail were both appearing at the summer market three years ago.
Later, they played gigs together.
O’Steen told me that his daughter does and sings her own music.
Prentiss was a nurse who worked for a number of different online companies.
Mike Shaffer, a bartender and intermittent entertainment organizer at The Julep Room near Gautier, said he last saw Lambi and Prentiss on the night of September 5, when Lamb had finished his guitar and harmonica performances for a sparse audience.
Nothing seemed to be wrong.
‘Only a happy couple,’ he said.
We messed around and threw tricks at each other.
So they were both quite clever.
Deceased teacher Schmidt led a first-year student seminar program and was specialized in Indian and colonial history, the school's English teacher Don Allan Mitchell said.
Schmidt was married and had three small children.
He studied at Emporia State University in Kansas and was the chairman of his fraternity and student board.
On Tuesday evening in Cleveland, the Delta State campus hosted a candle devotion attended by about 900 people, including teaching and other staff, students, and community members.
Schmidt's wife Liz and brother Jeff Schmidt also took part in the Mass, during which the university choir sang the songs Bright Morning Star and Amazing Grace.
Teaching will continue on Wednesday.
‘We’re trying to get our students back,’ LaForge said.
The crisis is over.
It's time for recovery.
Amy and Rogelio Solis are reporting Mississisipin's Cleveland.
Associated Press writer Emily Wagster Pettus from Missississipin Greenville and Rebecca Santana from New Orleans took part in the report.
The case has been corrected according to the fact that the cops now say Lamb made an emergency call after Schmidt's shooting, and that even though Lamb was referring to Prentiss as his wife during the call, there's no evidence that they were married.
Toys R Us plans to hire fewer employees for the holiday season
Toys R Us says it won't hire as many employees for the holiday season as it did last year, but retailers of toys and baby products say it's given current regulars and seasonal workers the opportunity to work more hours.
According to the company, it plans to hire 40 000 people to work in shops and distribution centers across the country, compared with 45 000 in the 2014 holiday season.
Most of the work is part-time.
The company said that interviews with applicants will begin this month, and the number of staff will rise from October to December.
Even though it's months before the holidays, the Christmas shopping season is approachinging, and companies are preparing to hire temporary workers to help occupy stores, as well as sell, ship and deliver products.
Michael J. Fox jokes about ‘dates’ with Princess Diana
The premiere of Back to the Future was a very important moment for Michael J. Fox professionally.
It was also noteworthy for personal reasons.
“We sat in the theatre waiting for the royals to arrive, and I realized that the seat next to me was empty, and then I realized Princess Diana’s place was next to me,” he said in a trailer for the upcoming documentary film Back in Time.
The movie begins and it occurs to me that I'm just a yawn and a hand-stretch away from a date with the Welsh princess.
Back in Time is a documentary about Back to the Future, interviewing Fox, Lea Thompson and Christopher Lloyd, as well as Robert Zemeckis and production director Steven Spielberg.
The return to the future premiered 30 years ago.
Spielberg said: “For me, it’s undoubtedly the finestest time travel movie of all time.”
Back in Time will be available as a video on request, as well as in some cinemas in October.
The U.N. leader says the situation in Syria will not be solved armedly
Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon’s response to Russia’s increased military support for Syria is ‘non-armed solution’ to this nearly five-year conflict, and additional weapons will only exacerbate violence and the suffering of millions of people.
Once again, the U.N. leader urged all parties involved, including the Divided United Nations Security Council, to reunite and to support non-discriminatory negotiations in order to find a political solution.
Ban told Wednesday's press conference that he plans to meet the foreign ministers of the five permanent Member States - the U.S., Russia, China, Britain and France - on a ministerial week of the General Assembly later this month to discuss Syria.
He regretted that the distribution of the Council, the Syrian people and the regional forces “has made the situation impossible to resolve.”
Ban urged five permanent Member States to demonstrate the solidarity and unanimity with regard to the Syrian crisis when dealinging, which they showed when agreeing on Iran's nuclear programme.
8 poll results that show that Donald Trump is serious
Some people have tried to label him a vanteer.
Others just think he's a joke.
Some are waiting to collapse.
While some Republicans have tried to get Donald Trump away from the top of support polls, it hasn't (yet) succeeded.
Of the most recent 11 national polls in ten, Donald Trump has been in the lead in a double-digit number, and some have begun to seriously consider what this means in terms of the real estate king's appointment possibilities.
Of course, the election period is still in its early stages.
These results don't mean that Trump is likely to become a Republican nominee.
Commentators point ou out that at this stage in 2011, Rick Perry was about to lose his lead to the rising Herman Cain, neither of which got a single state in the nomination process.
There are also many reasons why there would be problems for him in the general election.
However, external groups such as Jeb Bush's Super PAC and the Conservative Economic Organization Club for Growth have noted Trump's persistence and began using their funds to overthrow him.
The following recent poll results suggest that the real estate king isn't just a transient phase:
Trump's popularity figures have taken a complete turnaround.
Just before Donald Trump announced his candidacy in mid-June, Monmouth University's support polls showed that only two out of ten Republicans had a positive attitude towards the real estate king.
By mid-July, the figure was 40 per cent.
At the beginning of August, the figure was 52 per cent.
At the moment, six out of ten Republicans have a positive attitude towards Donald Trump.
About three in ten say they're negative.
These figures are also true in the first primary states.
Last week, a Quinnipiac University support poll in Iowa, 60 percent of Republicans said they'd welcome Trump.
Two-thirds of Republican voters would be pleased if Trump became a nominee.
In a CNN/ORC poll last week, 67 percent of Republicans said they'd either be “excited” or “satisfied” if Trump were a nominee.
Only two out of ten said they would be “shocked” if he became a candidate.
Only Ben Carson generates about the same level of enthusiasm as Trump (43 per cent “excited” vs. Trump’s 40 percent).
The next highest source of enthusiasm?
Marco Rubio by only 21%.
On the other hand, 47 percent of Republican voters would be ‘unhappy’ or ‘shocked’ if Jeb Bush, a favorite of those in power, became a candidate.
Trump's fiery nature isn't a problem for most Republicans.
Although Donald Trump has been widely criticized for his pompous speeches and insults, according to Monday's ABC News/Washington Post poll, 52 percent of Republican voters at the national level think the real estate king has a suitable character for the president.
According to Quinnipiac's survey last week's survey, the number in Iowa, which organizes the first convention, is the same: 52 percent of Republicans think Trump has the right character for a leader.
However, according to an ABC/Post poll, 44 per cent think that his character is not suitable for acting as president, and nearly six out of ten people think that his character does not belong to the White House.
Republican voters get used to the idea.
When Republican voters think like commentators, they think that Trump is a real option.
According to a CNN/ORC survey, four out of ten considered Trump the most likely option when asked who is most likely nominated a Republican nominee.
At the end of July, four out of ten supported Jeb Bush.
News release: Republican Party voters haven't always had access to the brightest crystal ball.
In the last parliamentary term, four out of ten Republicans at this time thought Rick Perry would be nominated, and only 28 percent supported the final candidate, Mitt Romney.
In any case, this shows that the majority of Republican voters consider the Trump campaign credible.
Even if Republicans were to support another candidate, Trump would still win almost everyone.
Some commentators have pointed out that the fragmented field is likely to have promoted Trump's leadership, as anti-Trumph support has been distributed among numerous other candidates.
However, a poll conducted by Monmouth University in early September showed that in a hypothetical bilateral race between Trump and almost all other Republican candidates, Trump receives majority support in almost all cases.
He leads Carly Fiorina with 13 points, Marco Rubio with 14 points, Walker with 15 points, Jeb Bush with 19 points and Rand Paul, John Kasichi and Chris Christie with 33 points.
He's tied with Ted Cruz.
Trump's only winning candidate?
If they were confronted, Ben Carson would beat businessman Trump with a big 18-point gap.
The vast majority of Donald Trump supporters have said they made the decision.
A new CBS/NYT poll on Tuesday shows that only a little more than half of the voters in favor of Trump have decided who their votes will go to.
Of course, the situation can change, and no one can say with absolute certainty that they will never change their mind.
46% said they might be able to exchange candidates.
In any case, Trump's biggest rival at the moment is another unlikely winner, neurosurgeon Ben Carson, but those voters who said they made the decision are twice as likely on Trump's side.
Six out of ten Republicans say they agree with Trump on immigration.
Ever since Donald Trump called Mexican immigrants ‘rapists’ in his campaign launch speech two months ago, immigration has been at the heart of the 2016 debate.
Some are concerned that Trump's flamboyance is pushing Latino voters further away from the Republican Party and injuring rebranding attempts.
According to a new ABC/Post survey released on Monday, six out of ten Republicans say they agree with Trump on immigration issues.
So it seems that as long as immigration is at the center of affairs, so is Donald Trump.
Frustration with the government has risen to a new peak.
Donald Trump's and Ben Carson's endorsement now accounts for about half of Republican voters, largely due to their status beyond politics.
Six out of ten Republicans said in Monday's ABC/Post poll that they'd rather choose someone outside of politics than one with government experience.
They're also angry at the federal government.
Two weeks ago, Des Moines Register/Bloomberg's survey of Iowa showed that three out of four Iowa Republicans are frustrated with Republicans in Congress: 54 percent are “unhappy” and 21 percent are “really angry.”
Jeremy Corbyn's first appearance during Prime Minister's Question Time
Corbyn's first appearance during Question Time has been eagerly awaited since his election
Jeremy Corbyn, the new leader of the Labour Labour Party, will face David Cameron for the first time during Prime Ministerial Question Time.
Corbyn asks about his first six questions shortly after noon, and it is likely that both the media and Labour MPs will closely watch over his performance.
Corbyn has demanded “less theatre and more facts” for this weekly screening.
He has also said that he could leave some of the events to his colleagues.
This meeting will be the first parliamentary test for Corbyn's leadership since he was appointed to the shadow government and speaking at an annual meeting of the trade union confederation on Tuesday.
At the same time, the Workers' Party leader's decision to stand quietly during the national anthem during Tuesday's memorial service, which took place on the 75th anniversary of the Battle for the British Air War, has attracted criticism among Conservative Party MPs and appeared in several front-page cases.
Corbyn's decision not to sing the national anthem has attracted attention
Corbyn’s spokesman said he ‘standed respectfully silently’ and that he acknowledged “the bravery of the Royal Air Force in the British Air War.”
Owen Smith, a member of Corbyn's shadow government, told BBC Two's Newsnight programme that he had urged the Labour leader to sing the national anthem, despite the fact that he felt that the Royal House should be abolished.
Nearly a dozen shadow ministers have declined to participate in Corbyn's inner circle, citing disagreements in economic, defence and foreign affairs. Initially, less than one-sixth of the parliamentary party supported him as leader.
BBC Political Correspondent Robin Brant says policy differences concerning the party’s position in the European Union and the government’s aid cap have ‘piled up’ within the Worker’s Party since Corbyn’s appointment.
Corbyn said at a meeting of the Confederation of Trade Unions that the Labour Party is proposing a change that would remove the entire roof.
Hours later in the shadow government's employment and pension secretary Smith stated that the party was “quite clear” in terms of simply opposing the government’s plans to lower the ceiling from 26 000 pounds to 23 000 pounds.
Corbyn will become the fifth Labour leader to be confronted by David Cameron over the past decade when he has been the leader of the Conservative Party.
The leader of the Labour Party has pledged a new approach to politics and now says that he has ‘crossified’ ideas into questions for the Prime Minister and has received more than 30 000 proposals.
Corbyn, MP for the Islington North region, has said that the Prime Minister's working hours are too confrontative and that he intends to abstain from both clever words and sniping, promising to focus on serious issues such as poverty, inequalities and the challenges of young people.
Corbyn has said that Angela Eagle's financial secretary of the shadow government will replace her during Question Times when Corbyn isn't attending, for example, when Cameron is abroad.
Corbyn has also suggested that other colleagues could sometimes be allowed to speak, and he has said that he approached the Speaker of the House of Commons, John Bercow.
When Cameron took the lead in 2005, he said he wanted to move further away from a political theatre, which is often linked to the prime minister's survey hours, but later had to admit his failure.
Since they began televised in 1990, the Prime Minister's Question Time has been an important measure in assessinging a leader's judgement, control of his lower house, and his position among other MPs, although critics have criticized Question Time that they've only become a travesty that requires far-reaching reforms.
Photographed in Joburg: Homeless young people trained as photographers
The center of Johannesburg is a tricky place to be homeless.
However, a group of former street children have found a way to learn skills and make a living.
‘I was shot in Joburg’ is a non-profit studio where homeless teenagers are taught how to take photos of their neighborhood and make a profit from it.
BBC News met with one of the first young graduates of the project.
