The value of the pound rises as the UK's wage growth accelerates
The value of the pound jumped upwards after the numbers showed an increase in UK wage growth, and at the same time the FTSE 100 index rose with SABMiller.
Between May and July, the UK's salaries, excluding bonuses, rose 2.9 per century compared to the same period last year.
The pace of growth was the fastest after 2009, and it was considered to bring with it estimates of the rise in prices.
The pound rose more than one percent against the dollar to 1.5505 dollars, and 0.84 percent against the euro to 1,3730 euros.
The FTSE 100 index closed at 1.49 per cent to 6229.21.
SABMiller was the most distinctive stock on the market and rose by 20 percent after by Rival AB InBev approached it with an offer.
The agreement would bring together two of the world's largest breweries and bring together brands such as Budweiser, Stella Artois, Peroni and Grolsch.
Burberry's stock rose 0.9 percent since the rivaling luxury group Richmont received encouraging news.
Richemont's latest sales figures were better than expected, and they also showed that sales in China - which is Burberry's key markets - had started to grow again.
Glencore's shares went up by 5 percent since the mining giant said it'd raised US$2.5 billion by issuing shares as part of its debt-cutting strategy.
Mondi’s shares in the packaging and paper group were the index’s largest decliners by 4.3 percent after Goldman Sachs changed its recommendations from the company to ‘sell’.
JD Sports rose by 3.1 per century after a sportswear company released information about its six-month revenue before taxes: an 83 per cent increase to 46.6 million pounds.
According to the head of JD Sports, higher salaries could damage the expansion
Peter Cowgill, president of JD Sports, says that if the minimum wage for employees is raised in the UK, it could mean that “the purchasing power of potential consumers is growing.”
However, this purchasing power is unlikely to outweigh the higher labour costs incurred by the company, Cowgill says.
The costs could go towards the company's expansion plans, he added, which would mean fewer additional jobs.
Metcash is launching a grassroots campaign against Aldi's intrusion
Metcash is trying to protect their IGA deals from Aldi's attack in South and Western Australia.
Metcash, a food wholesaler, has launched a grassroots campaign to defend IGA traders' market share in South and Western Australia ahead of Aldi's 700-million-dollar attack.
Aldi is preparing to open two distribution centers as well as the first store early next year in South and Western Australia with up to 120 deals. At the same time, Metcash and IGA supermarkets will strengthen their relationships with local food brokers and build the IGA brand through mainstream and social media marketing, as well as through events such as the Adelaide show.
IGA merchants and buyers have jumped in, and brands such as Simmo’s from Dunsborough offer a tasting in IGA’s stores, and store owners sell discounted tickets for Australian football matches to their regular customers.
Metcashin is expected to unveil further details of its defensive strategy on September 29, when this food, alcoholic and hardware wholesaler hosts an annual Investor Strategy Date.
There's a lot going on, as South and Western Australia together account for almost 30 percent of Metcash's IGA trade area, where yield margins are higher than Metcash's IGA network in the eastern Länders.
When it comes to shopping in South and Western Australia, visitors are famously parochial, and IGA retailers have a much higher market share than New South Wales, Victoria and Queensland: more than 30 percent in the south and 24 percent in the West compared to 14 percent in the Eastern countries.
Analysts such as Morgan Stanley, however, believe that Aldi grabs about 5 percent of the food market in these two states within 12 months of the opening of the first stores. Turnover is obtained from all operators, but mainly from independent retailers.
In today's report, Tom Kierath, an analyst at Morgan Stanley, estimates that IGA retailers could lose US$360 million each year on sale and Metcash could lose US$250 million by the end of 2016.
This forecast is based on the assumption that Aldi will open 25 stores in the south next year and 30 in the West next year, which will generate revenues of US$500 and US$600 million in these areas.
Loss of food and food sales and quantities at Metcash's high fixed costs base could cost Metcash approximately US$13 million in operating profit losses before interest and taxes, Kierath said.
At the same time, Metcash is losing market share in the eastern Länders mainly to Aldi and Coles, although a recent Roy Morgan study found that IGA's customers were among the most loyal.
Morgan Stanley expects Metcash's food and food sales to decline by 3.8 percent in 2016; 4.1 percent in 2017 and 2.1 percent in 2018, so that its market share drops from 14% in 2015 to 10.3 percent in 2020.
Hamish Douglass, chief investment fund manager and founder of Magella's Financial Group last week, said at a business lunch in Melbourne that Metcash could “well possibly lose in a decade.”
Metcash has declined to publicly respond to Douglass' comments.
However, it has been understood that a member of Metcash's board would have contacted Douglass last week.
Since 2010, IGA's market share has declined from 18% to 14%, and revenues from Metcash's food and food business, which delivers products to approximately 1200 IGA retailers, have declined by 43% since 2012. This reflects a reduction in debt financing for operations when sales and margins are under pressure.
However, the wholesaler holds its own and has invested more than US$150 million each year, lowering the prices of hundreds of products to the same level as Coles and Woolworths, helping IGA retailers renovate stores by adding faster-growing fresh food, as well as launching a better range of products from private brands.
Metcash CEO Ian Morrice says lower prices and upgrading stores have boosted attraction and he hopes that the wholesaler will return to profit growth in 2017, for the first time in four years.
Australian Tennis Federation President Steve Healy supports Thanasi Kokkinaki
Thanasi Kokkinakis deserves credit rather than criticism of his behavior
Thanasi Kokkinakis has suffered as a bystander in a recent storm that has been rampaging around his friend Nick Kyrgios. He deserves credit rather than criticism of his own behavior, says Steve Healy, president of the Australian Tennis Federation.
Kokkinakis, a younger member of the talented duo called ‘Special Ks, was involuntarily made public in a case where Kyrgios annoyed Stan Wawrinka in the Montreal Masters competition.
Shortly thereafter, in a fiery qualifier in Cincinnati, the head referee had to twice separate Kokkinakis and his opponent Ryan Harrison when Harrison declared: “Wawrinka should have beaten Kyrgios, and I would have to beat that brat.”
Although Kokkinakis was last seen to break his bat after a cramp ruiled his possible victory over Richard Gasquet at the US Open, Healy has been Kokkinakis' unwavering defender.
Right now, the young man in Glasgow is competing with Sam Groth for a second place in the doubles with Bernard Tomic in the Davis Cup semi-final against Britain.
‘I’m quite strongly of the opinion that Thanasi has just been dragged into a brawl,” Healy said.
He broken his bat when he lost because of a cramp at the US Open, but he's not the first player to break his bat when he's frustrated, and I know Thanas well enough to know he's not proud of it.
But he has behaved excellently well, even in quite provocative situations at the US Open with Harrison and so on, and I think he deserves compliments.
He has a great team around him, he has a great family around him, and I think he's been treated a little harshly.
He is very mature until the age of 19, he works diligently, fits into the team and so on, and has never had any problems with the other players. He is appreciated in the locker room, he trains hard, is in good shape and is quite a calm and ripe young man, and I think he should be judged according to his merits.
Healy has also advocated the decision not to include Kyrgios in important games starting on Friday, describing this ‘joint decision’ as a good solution for everyone, but still supporting the Canberran’s return to the team’s regular fleet.
“We want the team to be completely focused on the games and not all the controversy around [Kyrgios],” Healy said.
And Nick has risen really quickly, as Federer and Murray, for example, have pointed out, and he grows up in public, and has made some terrible mistakes and deserves all the criticism he receives. However, we are trying to support him, trying to help him understand the consequences of his actions, and I think it’s best for both the team and him if he spends some time out of the public eye to think about these consequences.
Europe's migration crisis: Hungary declares state of emergency, closes borders, arrests migrants
Hungary declared a state of emergency and closed its southern border against Serbia, arrestinging those who tried to enter the country illegally, with the aim of damming the influx of migrants entering the country.
There was chaos at the border as hundreds of migrants flocked to a neutral area and the Serbian authorities reacted with dismay.
People who fled the violence of their home countries have now set up their tents and, for an indefinite period of time, put themselves on a section of the road between the checkpoints of the two countries.
Frustration, however, increased.
As a police helicopter remained above them, the immigrants said, “Open the border!” and shouted insults to the Hungarian riot police.
Some people refused food and water to protest.
Serbia’s foreign minister declared that it was “impossible to accept” the return of migrants from Hungary as more and more from Macedonia and Greece.
‘(Serbia) wants to be part of the solution, not cause harm to civilians.
There will have to be a debate in the next few days with other countries and Brussels,” Ivica Dacic said in Prague.
The chaos on the Hungarian-Serbian border arose after a group of 28 nationalities failed to establish a common immigration policy at a contentious meeting in Brussels.
The ministers agreed on a division of responsibilities for 40 000 people seeking asylum, which have now taken over Italy and Greece. The ministers hopefully talked about reaching a final agreement - next month or by the end of next year - on which EU countries will take on 120 000 additional refugees, including part of Hungary.
German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Austria on Tuesday called for an extraordinary EU summit to discuss the continent's migration crisis.
However, Hungary does not hope that its neighbours or Brussels will act in the near future.
Tuesday's state of emergency in the two southern regions gave the authorities a greater power to take action in the crisis and made it possible for you to be shut down and asylum cases to be speeded up.
At the same time, Hungarian Foreign Minister Peter Szijjarto said that now that the fence on the Serbian border is ready, the country also intends to set up a barbed-wired fence on part of its border with Romania in order to halt the influence of immigrants through the country.
CBS News correspondent Charlie D’Agata reported that even prisoners had been recruited to finish the barbed wire fence between Hungary and Serbia.
Over the past few months, Hungary has become the largest bottleneck and entry point in the European Union for refugees, many of whom are fleeing the war from Syria and the rest of the Middle East.
However, Prime Minister Viktor Orban has argued that the majority of arrivals are seeking a better life, rather than those fleeing the war, who are entitled to protection - this view strongly contradicts other EU countries, including Germany.
The new laws, which came into force at midnight in Hungary, now define an attempt to surpasse or damage a 4-metre (13 foot) barbed-wire fence built by the government on the southern border with Serbia, as well as including longer prison sentences for convicted traffickers.
“Due to the situation caused by mass immigration, the Hungarian government declares a state of emergency,” government spokesman Zoltan Kovacs told reporters in Szeged.
We are very clear on the matter: Crossing an illegal border is a crime.
Parliament still formally has to approve the positioning of the troops expected for next week, but Associated Press reporters at the border have seen heavily armed military personnel with vehicles and dogs for days.
Orban's domestic security adviser Gyorgy Bakondi said authorities had apprehended 45 people as they tried to cross the border, as well as 15 who had already reached the country.
They got over by damaging the fence, are now jailed by the police, and they are accused of violations as defined in the new laws.
Authorities are repairing the fence quickly.
On Tuesday morning, Hungarian officials also closed two of the seven crossing points on the border with Serbia after they sent a barbed-wired train carriage to close one of those places.
Roszke, the main border crossing, near Roszke in Hungary, was covered in chaos when the Hungarians opened a small office in a metal container for handling people and the crowds tried to shove inside.
About 20 got in, but thousands were left outside.
A group of immigrants blocked the main highway between Serbia and Hungary, saying they would refuse food and drink until they are allowed to come to Hungary.
According to a national survey, Hillary Clinton's lead over Bernie Sanders has been narrowed by half
Former Secretary of State Hillary Clinton will take the lead in a two-digit Democrat nomination contest, but Senator Bernie Sanders clinches him.
Clinton is now backed by 47 percent of Democratic voters (decreased from 58 percent), and the second-placed Sanders is backed by 27 percent (from 17 percent).
Vice President Joe Biden has not yet announced whether he will join the presidential race, but he is supported by 15 percent of Democratic voters.
More than five months before the start of the primaries, the majority of Democrat voters state that it is still too early to say that they would have decided who they were voting for.
Clinton's supporters, however, stand firmer behind their own choices than supporters of other candidates.
Clinton's popularity has declined among several population groups since the CBS News Poll survey last month, and at the same time Sanders has risen somewhat.
Clinton is strongly in the lead among women and moderates, but her leadership has narrowed.
His popularity among men has declined considerably, and Sanders is only 5 percentage points behind.
Clinton's lead among democrats under the age of 50 has been lost, and she's now tied with Sanders.
Clinton, however, clearly leads among older voters.
Last month, Clinton leaded Sanders by a double-digit number among liberals, but now Sanders leads this group by five percentage points.
About half of the country's Democrat voters say they're enthusiastically supporting Clinton if she becomes the party's nominee.
A quarter say they'd support him with certain reservations, and 15 percent would only support him because he's a candidate.
Six percent wouldn't support him at all.
It's not very surprising that Clinton's current supporters are very likely eager to support her.
Opinions are more confusing for those who do not support Clinton at the moment - only a quarter would enthusiastically support him.
Whereas 48 percent of Democrat voters say they'd enthusiastically support Clinton, Republican number one Donald Trump is said by fewer Republican voters (35 percent).
When supporters of Clinton were askered in the open question why they wanted her to be a candidate, the most popular answer was that she had the appropriate experience (16 percent). Since then, the most popular answers were that now is the right time for a female president (13 percent) and that she is the best candidate for the position (10 percent).
Clinton's supporters also mentioned his connection to Bill Clinton (9%).
The same opinions, as well as the possibility of being elected, were also mentioned.
Since the vice-president influences a possible presidential candidacy, 57 percent of Democratic voters would like to see him already involved in the race - even if a third disagree.
When askered from Democratic voters who they would choose second to the party nomination, Biden is the most popular choice.
More than half of Clinton supporters say they would choose Biden, which is much more popular than Sanders.
If Biden decides not to run for a Democratic nomination, Hilary Clinton's lead over Sanders is growing. If Biden goes along, the lead will be 20 percentage points, but if he opts out, it'll be 30.
55 percent of Democrat voters believe Clinton has the best chances to win the presidential election, but this figure has declined from 78 percent last month.
By this standard, Biden falls far away, but the proportion of those who consider him to be most voted has increased.
However, when questioned democrat voters what was the most important thing in the candidate, they put their opinions ahead of voting.
Similarly, last month, no Democratic candidate or potential candidate was considered an impossible candidate for these voters.
Democrat candidates or potential candidates are doing well on certain key issues among their party's voters.
The majority consider Clinton, Sanders and Biden to be honest, trustworthy and strong leaders.
Biden fares best among the trio in honesty, while Clinton's strength is leadership.
30 percent of Democrat voters say they don’t think Clinton is honest and trustworthy, which is twice as much as Sanders (15 percent), but 27 percent say they don’t have an opinion about Sanders.
Democrat voters also generally argue that Clinton, Sanders and Biden ideologically agree.
Looking at all of these issues, about a quarter of Democrat voters say they don't have an opinion about Sanders.
Althoughous Hillary Clinton's personal e-mail address and server use while she's Secretary of State still raises questions, the majority of Democratic voters are pleased with her explanation of the issue, saying it didn't affect their general opinion about him.
However, it's different when you look at America as a whole.
Half of Americans are now dissatisfied with his explanation, and only about a third are satisfied.
People's opinions were divided in March shortly after the issue came to light.
The majority of Americans say that their overall opinion of Clinton wasn't influenced by e-mail polemics, even though 36 percent say their opinion of him has deteriorated.
More than six out of ten Republicans say their opinions about Clinton have gotten worse.
Although Clinton's own party voters consider him honest and trustworthy, this is still a problem for him among all registered voters. Only 32 percent consider him honest and trustworthy, and this figure has fallen by 8 percentage points since last month, as well as 15 percentage points since May.
62% of registered voters say they don't think Clinton is honest and trustworthy.
At the same time, 55% of the country's registered voters also don't consider Trump honest and trustworthy.
Both Clinton and Trump are considered good leaders.
Of the six candidates included in the study, Biden, Carson and Bush are considered the most honest.
As a rule, Sanders is considered honest, but not many people have an opinion about him, just like Carson.
The margin of error in a study involving 351 Democrat voters is 6 percentage points.
Ben Carson is approaching Donald Trump in a national survey
Ben Carson comes close to Donald Trump in the latest CBS News/New York Times poll
In the new poll, two candidates collected half of all the votes, leaving the others to fight for a foothold.
Republican voters across the country are still strongly in favor of Trump, whose endorsement has risen from 23 per cent of the previous CBS poll, published last month's Fox News election debate, to 27 per cent on Tuesday.
At the same time, however, Carson resulted from 6% to 23%.
The survey also found that Carson is doing well with various demographics and is ousting Trump among collegeed Republican voters.
Carly Fiorina and Ohion Governor John Kasichi have lasted the rest of the field. Support for Wisconsin Governor Scott Walker has fallen the most: from 10 percent in the last month to 2 percent.
Former Florida Governor Jeb Bush, former Arkansas Governor Mike Huckabee and Florida Senator Marco Rubio are ranked in third place at 6%.
The margin of error in the 376 Republican voters of this latest survey conducted in September is +/- 6 per cent.
The latest CNN/ORC poll released last week showed Carson is on the rise in polls: he has 19 percent support among Republicans, and Trump is ahead of him with 32 percent.
According to the latest ABC News/Washington Post poll, Trump was significantly ahead of Carson.
As Carson's popularity rose last week, Trump criticized retired neurosurgery in vain, even though Carso apologized for questioning Trump's faith.
‘We need energy,’ Trump said last Saturday during a campaign visit to Iowa.
Five fraternity members are accused of Baruch College's first-year student's death
Nearly two years after a student died in a fraternity hazing ceremony in the Pocono Mountains district of Pennsylvania, a murder charge has been filed against five people.
Members of the Pi Delta Psi Brotherhood at Baruch College in New York City brutally attacked Chun “Michael” Deng during a rite of membership in September 2013, and then stalled to take him to the hospital, thus contributing to his death.
Authorities say Deng's eyes were blindfolded and his back was loaded with more than 13 kilograms full of sand, after which he was repeatedly tackled to the ground as he tried to walk through an icy field towards the paint.
Deng finally complained that his head hurts, after which he lost consciousness.
Brotherhood members did not call the emergency number after Deng fainted, but, according to the accusations, waited for an hour before bringing him to the hospital, police said.
However, they had time to exchange Deng's clothes, look online for information about his symptoms, and contact Andy Meng, the brotherhood's national president, asking him for advice.
‘At this point, members began to hide their ritual equipment and, therefore, in practice, prioritized the brotherhood’s well-being over Michael Deng,” police chief Wagner said.
They couldn't get Deng to come back, and he died the next morning.
According to the autopsy protocol, Deng received repeated blunt object blows to the head, body and thighs.
‘Too many families have been subjected to fraternity hazing, with at least one student having died each year as a result since 1970,” Doug Fierberg, a lawyer representing the Deng family, said in a statement.
Brotherhoods and their members must be held accountable, and this step of the authorities is important.
A grand jury in Monroe County advocated that the first five members will be charged with third-degree murder
32 other members of the Brotherhood, including Meng, are also prosecuted for violent hazing and criminal intrigue.
‘We don’t want to be held accountable only by individuals. If there’s an organisation behind what’s happened that endorsed the situation either formally or informally, they should also be held accountable,” Wagner said at Tuesday’s press conference.
Baruch College forbade the Brotherhood and the National Brotherhood unleashed its connection to the local branch.
This is what happens when the U.S. Federal Reserve raises interest rates
Raised interest rates will come, and the market for rising prices will fall, bond yields will rise and the economy will have to recesse.
Well, that's what we'll know.
What we don't know is how long it's gonna take or how long it's going to take.
The past doesn't give much help when it comes to the economy.
The recession has taken only 11 months since the first withdrawal, sometimes it has been as long as 86 months.
The U.S. central bank's aggression in raising interest rates has often been, though not always, a defining factor in how the economy and financial assets react.
That's why U.S. Central Bank officials have so vigorously stressed that investors shouldn't focus on it because interest rates are starting to be raised, but mainly on the trajectory of the period of normalization of the situation.
Indeed, there are several variables in the equation.
In the end, however, market participants may find that any fuss related to the rate hike has been excessive.
“The central bank’s first post-global economic increase will inevitably be interpreted in some circles as the end of a ‘cheap-cheat’ era,” said Julian Jessop, chief economic expert on global affairs at Capital Economics.
On the other hand, we do not expect the gradual return of U.S. interest rates to normal, albeit still low, the level that many seem to be afraid of.
However, this does not mean that it does not have an impact.
Below is an overview of how some areas of the economy will react on the basis of previous developments:
As has been seen on the market in the last month or so, waiting to raise interest rates can make the situation unstable.
When an increase becomes a fact, the effect isn't quite that dramatic after all.
‘It seems that the return on capital tends to stagnate for 12 to 24 months after the first increase, which in turn potentially reflects a lag in monetary policy,” Deutsche Bank analysts said in a recent study on what happens after a central bank raise.
More specifically, in the last 35 years, the market tends to be on a strong high of around 14 per cent when approaching the increase, 250 days after that the situation is fairly steady (an average increase of 2.6 percent), and the normal state has been reached after 500 days with an average return of 14.4% in the last six cycles, according to a recent analysis by Bob Doll, a leading capital strategist at Nuveen Asset Management.
Deutche Bank says the impact on stocks tends to be more pronounced at a later stage of the upgrade cycle when returns start to decline.
Recessions are part of the economy, but interest rates often contribute to their arrival.
In the current situation, the central bank is facing certain challenges that have not existed in the past and which can accelerate the recession.
In particular, gross domestic product, which will be the closestest of its all-time level before the central bank's interest rate hike.
According to Deutsche Bank's 118 increases since 1950, the nominal annual GDP has been less than 4.5 per cent.
Although, in the second quarter of 2015, the figure was 3.6 per cent, few believe it will remain, while the third quarter has progressed by 1.5 percent, according to the Atlanta Federal Reserve Bank.
Raising interest rates in such a fragile economic situation may be risky and raise the question of ‘whether this time will be different,’ Deutsche Bank said.
Market participants have been preparing for a central bank raise throughout the year, and everyone is awaiting this week's meeting of the Federal Open Market Committee, which ends on Thursday.
While many strategists and financial experts believe the committee can accept the increase at this meeting, futures trading predicts only a 25 percent probability.
“Our study shows that, since 1950, all the upgrade cycles have so far emerged in supercycles of increasing leverage, where GDP has surpassed its pre-recession peaks very quickly after the stimulus has begun,” the report said.
By contrast, there has now been an exceptionally slow recovery from the recession, which has been the worst of the observation period.
In today's cycle, the central banker has waited by far the longest from the end of the last recession: the record has been 35 months, and now it's the 74th month.
Fixed returns have also been unstable while the market is awaiting interest rate hike, and the model is somewhat similar to perceptions of capital.
The main difference is that when the central bank changes its policy, its effects are reflected more quickly in bonds than in stocks.
The direction of the proceeds of the bonds appears to change immediately at the first increase/cut of the cycle.
At the end of the upgrade cycle, bond yields will immediately decrease,” Deutche Bank said.
Charles Schwab strategists believe that raising interest rates will lead to the convergence of longer- and shorter-term bond yields, thus smoothing the yield curve.
High-yielding bonds often perform better in such a situation, although “we’re still cautious when it comes to looking for a return,” said Kathy Jones, Schwab’s fixed-income analyst.
Surprisingly enough, Jones believes that the market has already adapted to the central bank's policy expectations.
“In addition to dollar strength and a drop in inflationary expectations, there are a number of indications that markets have adapted to the prospects for tighter monetary policy,” he wrote.
Short-term interest rates are high, the yield curve is smoother, credit margins have increased and volatility has increased - all of these are characteristic of the markets when the central bank has tightened its policy.
In general, companies whose entrepreneurial activities are mainly located in the United States win when interest rates rise and local products become more attractive.
Multinational corporations that have a lot of debt will perform worse when an increase in the value of the dollar makes their products more expensive on the world market and their debts become more expensive than they have financing.
“History shows that ‘high-quality’ stocks tend to be more lucrative in the three months after the first interest rate hike,” Goldman Sachs analysts said in a report addressed to customers.
Companies with a strong turnover surpassed weak turnover companies by an average of five percentage points after the increases in 1994, 1999 and 2004.
Enterprises with a high return on capital gains and low volatility shares performed better than lower-quality companies. The average for the former was 4 (percentage points) and that of the latter 3 (percentage points).
There's going to be a big problem.
“Those companies with a lot of floating-interested debt are losing the most,” Goldman said.
Outside of stock market speculation, consumers also have the opportunity to benefit from an increase in the value of the dollar.
Also, savers can make a profit due to higher returns, although experts disagree on how quickly the impact will be felt.
BBC Radio is a copy of Classic FM, MP Andrew Bingham says
Conservative MP Andrew Bingham accuses ‘comprehension’
He argues that it becomes more and more difficult to separate it from a commercial competitor
In addition, he targeted the BBC Music Awards, saying that they only mimicked the Brit Awards
Damien Collins criticized The Voice, saying it wasn't original either
BBC Radio 3 is ‘turning’ into Classic FM, claims a leading MP, adding to the production company’s pressure to prove that it is doing something that commercial production companies don’t do.
Conservative MP Andrew Bingham also told BBC executives that a public service radio station seemed to “gain popular awareness” of its content, and that it is becoming increasingly difficult to distinguish it from a commercial rival.
‘It seems that Radio 3 – I wouldn’t want to use the term ‘comprehension’ – is about to become Classic FM,” he said.
He also targeted the BBC Music Awards, which was launched last December.
“Last year’s BBC Music Awards just look likely for the Brit Awards under a different name.
So if you’re trying to do something different, here are the two direct examples I’ve remembered when you’re just copying something that’s already on the market,” Bingham said.
The BBC's Director-General denied the charges, claiming that BBC Radio 3 is far ahead of its commercial rival when it comes to live music and sharing information with citizens.
Radio 3 teaches things about classical music that I can't learn anywhere else.
Its commitment to live music and music is, in fact, first-class.
“In addition, I think it’s really important that Radio 3 is committed to discovering new music and ordering new works,” he said.
Earlier this year, the head of BBC Radio 3, Alan Davey, argued that the channel needs to do more work than in the past to reachience the audience, as Britons know less about classical music.
However, Radio 3 was not the only part of the BBC's offering, which was criticised for stepping on the toes of commercial competitors during the hearing of the Committee on Culture, Media and Sport.
Damien Collins, a member of the Conservative Tory Party, criticized The Voice, a music competition that the BBC bought from the Dutch production company Talpa Media for 22 million pounds.
Do you understand why this programme is often debated because it is not original and is by no means original?
Lordi Hall argued that this BBC1 programme, starring Paloma Faith and Boy George, has been produced ‘quite like the BBC.’
He added that he wants the production company to “discover the success inside his own house” the next time when it embarks on an entertainment series.
However, he refused to prevent the production of the upcoming The Voice seasons, or to stipulate the money of the licensees to be spent on other entertainment formats.
Online sales rose the least so far in August, Black Friday may be the cause
Online sales rose by 7 per cent in the first three months of 2015
Last month was the lowest since the statistics began in 2000
Three years ago, online sales were boosted by the use of mobile devices to make purchases
Experts believe November's Black Friday could curb consumption
Making virtual purchases has been growing tremendously for more than a decade and has transformed many of Britain's shopping streets into haunted cities.
Experts, however, say there are now indications of an equilibriation of online sales in the air, with the latest figures showing only a 7% increase for the first quarter of 2015.
Total consumption in August grew the least since the start of the statistics in 2000: only five per cent rise from last year.
Experts say there are indications in the air that online sales are offset when the most recent figures showed an increase of only 7 percent for the first quarter of 2015.
“If you look at the growth levels of previous years and go back to 2000, it was clear that the situation would become more reasonable over time,” Andy Mulcahy, JMRG sales index reporter, told the Guardian.
He explained that three years ago, online sales were boosted when buyers began using their mobile phones to make purchases.
It meant that more people shopped online, and that they could be done in different environments, such as on the couch.
But now it's smoothing, and (growth) can decrease.
Black Friday has been seen as one of the reasons behind the sluggishing trades.
Experts believe that consumers will curb shopping ahead of November's last Friday event.
Last year, buyers five times more than the usual average flocked to their computers to snatch stores, knocking out the websites of many merchants.
Experts believe that Black Friday, which caused buyers to quarrel about the discoveries, could curb shopping
A study published by the British Retail Consortium found that only about one pound of five were spent online.
In the case of certain products, such as televisions and washing machines, more than a third of purchases are now made from online stores.
At the same time, more than a quarter of people buy clothes and shoes online instead of using fashion chains for shopping streets.
Muslims buried those who died in Mecca's crane accident, Bin Laden's family
Muslims carried the bodies of 29 deceased across the streets during the first funeral in Mecca
Saudi Arabia has partly blamed the collapse of the construction giant Saudi Binladin Group
According to the Ministry of Health, a total of 394 people were also injured when the crane collapsed
29 the dead were carried in the air through the streets when hundreds of Muslims gathered to bury them. The victims died when the crane collapsed at the Grand Mosque of Mecca.
Primarily dressed in white, mourners filled the route to honor the first of the dead who made their last trip to Al-Moaissem cemetery.
Just a few days ago, a crane fell through the roof of the mosque and knocked down worshippers.
Bodies were released for burial when the King of Saudi Arabia confirmed that the Bin Laden family's construction group was culprited by a catastrophe in which 394 people were also injured.
Of the worshippers killed by a crane killed in the Mecca Grand Mosque, 29 bodies were carried across the streets
Among the dead were Qasim Akram Bolton, the father of four, from the Manchester region. He was on his first pilgrimage when the crane collapsed.
Akram had been to the Grand Mosque with his parents before the start of the annual hajj pilgrimage.
The Saudi authorities have already sworn that Hajj to Mecca will continue when thousands constantly arrive in the city.
Today, King Salman stated that the Binladin Group should not have left the crane's stalk when it wasn't in use.
A decision published by the state-ruled Saudi Press Agency in Arabic said Binladin Group was partly to blame for the collapse.
This detail was not mentioned by the English-language service of the news agency, but it stated that the company's directors are prohibited from travelling abroad.
Earlier this week, Salman swore to find out what caused the crane's crane to fall into the grand mosque's yard, where hundreds of thousands of Muslims had gathered beforeprising this month's Hajj pilgrimage.
Binladin Group has not published notices about the crane's collapse, and its representatives have not been contacted for comment.
The royal decision also accused the group of not using up-to-date protective measures and did not cooperate with meteorological authorities.
There are dozens of cranes around Mecca's Grand Mosque, which are part of the huge building grove that Binladin Group leads.
Binladin's family has been close to the Saudian royal family for decades, leading major construction projects.
Al-Qaeda's late leader Osama bin Laden was a rebel boy whose family banned in the 1990's.
The collapse occurred in windy and rainy weather just days before millions arrive in the kingdom for a pilgrimage that every capable Muslim should do at least once in their life.
Authorities say 158 people are still in hospital.
Authorities ordered one million rials (174 000 pounds) to be paid to the relatives of the deceased and those permanently injured.
The other injured will receive half of this amount.
The B&Q boss says that Eastern European workers working cheaply are behind the popularity
“Britains are increasingly using auxiliary services,” Veronique Laury said
The number of professionals from Eastern Europe is behind the rise
As a result, 200 more Screwfix stores for professionals will be opened
At the same time, Kingfisher will close 60 B&Q stores across the country
Author Rupert Steiner, the city's chief correspondent for the Daily Mail
The British are said to give up hammers and paintbrushes and rather hire skilled Eastern European workers to carry out home renovation work
It seems that if you're nifty with a hammer or you're beating to paint walls, you're in the minority these days.
“Britons are hiring more and more auxiliaries to do-it-yourself work in their homes,” said B&Q’s boss.
Veronique Laury, the new CEO of Kingfisher, which owns the home renovation chain, said the popularity is due to the number of skilled workers from Eastern Europe and the fact that they offer their services at a mock price.
This will result in Kingfisher planning to open 200 new Screwfix stores for workers and closing 60 B&Q stores.
Laury said she wants more women to work in B&Q stores, because eight out of ten purchasing decisions made in the store are specifically made by a more beautiful sex.
Today, most of the staff are men.
He said that changes in the population must be taken into account: Today, there are more one-woman households where more home renovation work is carried out than before.
Laury said, “Most of the staff in our stores are still men.
We need to seek a better balance.
However, half of our customers are women, and 80 per cent of product purchasing decisions are made by women.
Women play a big role in the decision-making process.”
Laury is one of only the five women who run the UK's FTSE100 index. She's rebuilt her management team with an equal number of male and female superiors.
Kingfisher is almost the only company in whose group is exactly 50 per cent women, including its CEO and Chief Financial Officer.
Laury said, “This was a conscious decision – diversity is an important issue here.
It hasn’t been difficult to find good women on the management team.”
Kingfisher reported a decrease in both six-month proceeds and sales after the group took over the cost of closing stores.
As a result of the trend, Kingfisher, which owns Screwfix and B&Q stores, is about to open 200 new stores
Three ace governments decided to resort to compulsive legislation in order to save the country's economy.
Visible effects were obtained quite quickly.
SAK's and other employees' central organisations' sharp end began to be forced immediately, and as a result, the country will stand on Friday due to an extensive demonstration by employees.
A lot of people think there's also reason.
Let's ask if it's worth going on strike when it's going bad anyway.
A resistance movement has even been set up.
More than 50 000 people on the Internet have already announced that they are not going to take part in the demonstration, but are going to work.
In the old good consensus times, this would have been impossible.
Of course, there was no internet to protest, but openly declaring itself a scrumb would have been the political suicide of a small person.
The protest of a few tens of thousands of tens of trade unions does not matter much, but it gives cause for thought.
The position of the trade union movement is no longer as self-evident as it was twenty years ago.
Not everyone knows the goals of the movement as their own, values have changed
From the point of view of the attractiveness of the activities of the trade union movement, the Government's drastic solutions can also be seen as an outright gift of heaven
When things have seemed to roll on their own weight for years, now the unions are finally getting to the point, i.e. defending the rights of the employee, visibly and properly.
In this way, the government may want to strengthen the role of the trade union movement, as membership in the trade union is perhaps seemed more necessary than before.
Let's no matter what we think about the actions of the organisations, it's nonsense that Friday's demonstration would somehow stop Finland.
Finland stopped a long time ago.
For example, industrial output has already declined for 31 months in a row.
Gross domestic product will continue to decline, and unemployment will rise.
You'd think these things would force everyone's head.
The University of Lappeenranta's austerity: Aiming to save 8.5 million, personnel to be reduced by 130
The Lappeenranta University of Technology is launching an austerity, the aim of which aims to reduce costs by a total of 8.5 million euros.
As part of the savings, the university will start co-operation negotiations concerning the entire personnel, with a savings target of 6.5 million annually.
In practice, this means a reduction of about 130 employees.
In addition, two million will be saved from other activities.
The co-operation negotiations are based on the 2016 budget, which predicts that the actual operations will have a deficit of 8.5 million.
It is also influenced by government budget cuts.
In this financial year, the University will make a deficit of approximately 2.6 million.
Expenses will increase slightly for next year, but at the same time the funding received from the ministry will be reduced by 2 million euros and other funding by 3.2 million euros.
The University's total budget is about 85 million euros, so the savings target is 10 per cent of the total budget.
The combined effect of massive cuts in teaching and research, as well as the decrease in business funding, is very significant for our university, says Rector Anneli Pauli in a press release.
Before the summer, the effects of the cuts seemed even more moderate, but now the decrease in supplementary funding is reflected in new proportions.
Negotiations with shop stewards will be launched on Tuesday next week and will last for six weeks prescribed by law.
Onnibus wants Finland on the railways
Sir Brian Souter, the owner of the low-cost bus company Onnibus, tells Yle that the company is also aiming for rail traffic in Finland.
I'm very interested in getting involved in Finland's rail traffic.
