David Cameron knows Lorax better than that, don't you?
Every parent will soon learn something: if your child loves a book, it won’t be long before they learn it almost by heart.
Unless you're David Cameron.
When asked the Prime Minister about his favorite children's book, he chose Dr Seussi's The Lorax, an environmental story published in 1971.
The choice was tricky, Cameron writes in a post published on SuperCamps’s website and continues: “Fun, moving, creative and contains a strong message. I enjoyed reading this book to my children because it always seems to have a picture or message that we haven’t seen before.”
All of this is true, but the next chapter, in which Cameron supposedly describes the plot of the book, is a summary of the 2012 film version, in which new characters and details had been supplemented with a fairly short original.
‘Within the walls of the city of Tarville, where all nature has vanished and even the air is merchandisery, the Ted boy hopes to win the heart of the girl of his dreams by fulfilling Audrey’s wish to see the real tree,” the text says and describes a love story not written by Dr Seuss, who died in 1991.
If this isn’t yet suspicious enough, Cameron’s text sounds in some places as if it’s taken from the Lorax movie plot summary found on the Internet Movie Database website, which begins as follows: “In the city of Tarville inside the walls, where everything is artificial and even the air is merchandised, the Ted boy hopes to win the hearts of the Audrey girl of his dreams.”
Considering that Cameron is publicly an active parent, it seems unlikely that he would be so unfamiliar with the plot of the book. In it, a young, nameless boy hears how the local nature has been destroyed by a greedy, tree-beating and need-weaving Kerskanen, ignorancing the story of the short, moustached creature Lorax, who “talks for the trees.”
Such a relatively invisible PR task is probably outsourced to the assistant, who apparently has never read the book 20 times in a row for a child.
A spokesman for the Prime Minister said the text must have come from Cameron's constituency office.
SuperCamps confirmed that the text was received from Cameron's staff, but did not comment further at that moment.
