Britain's departure from the EU (Brexit) can take the profit if the economy is in difficulty in 2017
David Cameron’s half-baked renegotiation strategies to keep Britain in the EU get into bigger and bigger problems every day, even if it doesn’t take into account even the heartfelt Jeremy ‘Is it yes or no?’ Corbyn’s election to the leadership of the Workers’ Party or the indignation of the trade union confederation to Europe on Tuesday.
Foreigners also have their own Corbyn and Nigel Farage.
A European diplomatic friend recently explained that, since the EU is so deep in various crises, not all of which are its own fault, some Member States are tired of Britain's self-centered agendament, which is full of dissenting concessions and disturbancing treaty changes, which these states largely do not want.
In its annual meeting in Brighton, the Confederation of Trade Unions decided that it did not want them if they erode social protection.
For example, we could call it Dave's and George's selfie agenda.
The Prime Minister and the Minister of Finance are late for summits or bilateral meetings in foreign capitals, taking a picture of themselves in front of the famous local memorial, either with or without a local glitcher, and then come home, saying they win supporters for the reforms.
It's all about me.
My European friend, who wants an out-of-the-top Britain within an EU tent - ‘Don’t leave us alone with France,’ they say from Berlin – suggests that the short-sightest ministers and civil servants in certain capitals are now at such a point that they won’t care we’ll then leave.
Germany's cautious and moderate Chancellor Angela Merkel is an ally, but she's been in power for 10 years and wants to leave her actions before it gets ruined in the same way it happened to Thatcher (11 years), Mitterrand (14) and Kohl (16).
François Hollande of France is facing re-election or compensation in 2017.
He's a fading force, too.
Poland could soon have a nationalist right-wing government, Spain has a left-winger.
It is difficult to see these countries sacrificing on behalf of selfie Brits, when their energy should rather be directed to prevent Scotland from leaving the United Kingdom - they don't wait it either because of their own separatist tendencies.
So, my friend suggests, some such ministers may even consciously plan making useless remarks in the style of the Scottish National Party during Britain's 2017 referendum campaign to speed up the Brexit process.
‘Re-negotiations were an organized case’ and ‘Renegotiations had no significance for Britain.’
Europeans have had a bitter experience of how greedy Fleet Street's press can be and how EU-skeptical the autocrats of the press are, what they were not at the time of the 1975 referendum inspired by Tony Benn, when our membership, which began in 1973, was strengthened by a ratio of 2:1.
They can vaguely remember Boris Johnson, the largest Euro-opportunist who made a name for himself by typing ‘EU direct bananas’ when he was a Brussels correspondent in the early 90’s.
Ed Miliband may have been a fool, but at least he knew who his enemies were.
They included a diverse group of tax-guantéed rogues, foreigners hacking phones, and people whose domicile is nominally elsewhere and who own most of our major newspapers.
Tony Blair actually thought exactly the same about them, but realized that if you're in a cage with a lustful gorilla, you'll have to give up the bananas for deception, unless you'd want to be on that gorilla's lunch menu and later on dinner.
In fact, Miliband was more Blairian than Blair himself when it comes to opposing Cameron's renegotiation trick, because Bair had agreed to the principle behind the EU's rejected constitution, which was rejected by French and Dutch voters in 2005, thus giving Blair a way out of the situation.
The press never forgived him either.
But the nice Jeremy Corbyn, who kindly clapped me in the back at Westminster's House of Parliament on Monday (I told him he doesn't have time to waste on me anymore) seems to be in danger of reactivating Neil Kinnocki's 80's boycotting Murdochia strategy, which was a mistake both then and now.
However, even though it may take time, he is forced to develop a coherent strategy to keep in touch with the media, not me, but without a doubt Andrew Marr, from whose BBC couch Corbyn left on Sunday.
Corbyn's official position in Europe is, to put it beautifully, evolving.
He says he would like us to stay in a reformed EU.
Well, the majority of people outside the British Independence Party Ukippi's warhead enthusiasm, as well as the fiercest nationalists in the Conservative Party, can certainly put themselves behind this opinion.
Hilary “Benn, but not a Benn fan,” Benn said this both on the radio and on TV this week, when her inner scout would have wanted to admit that Corbyn’s choice to appoint John McDonnell as the finance secretary of the shadow government was a bad idea.
It reminded me of the 70's, when his father-in-law struggled on TV, trying to be disloyal towards his labour party's governing colleagues without losing his seat in the government.
Hilary's efforts were more attractive.
However, the fight to keep up in a ‘situation anywhere’ — Benn’s statement—wasn’t what McDonnell said, and just by all that Corbyn told the Peers and MPs at a private meeting on Monday night.
Whatever the unhierarchical, emotional and personal image that Corbyn wants to convey (remember that he even touched me), the discipline of communication is important in the era of round-the-clock television, and especially in the swirls of social media, which is a favorable breeding ground for paranoia and deception.
As McDonnell has said, it's only fair not to give Cameron a free ticket to Europe by allowing him to take the support of the Workers' Party in the referendum campaign to be self-evident.
At least Simon Jenkins thinks so.
But McDonnell and Corbyn's basic twirling around EU membership (and being in line with their Benn-spirited regulated economy and anti-capitalist past) is no longer just an inconvenient discussion among left-wingers, for whom narcissism of small discrepancies is a central dog in doctrinal squabbling.
As far as his duality towards NATO (Tom Watson is convinced that Corby won't campaign to give up), foreign diplomats just have to try to explain it to their superiors.
Why should Paris or Berlin waste political capital (they have suspicious voters too) concessions to Britain when it could be completely pointless?
The United States is already somewhat disappointed by the poor British military performance in Iraq, Libya and Syria, even with a cost-cutting Conservative government in rule, as I was reminded at the start of Monday's British Influence Security Forum.
Experts Criticize Public Health England e-cigarette assessment
Investigators have said the state-funded agency's claim that e-cigarettes are 95% less harmful than the traditional ones sparked a 12-person meeting where some had links to the tobacco industry.
Experts writing for the British Medical Journal (BMJ) joined Lancet in criticizing the evidence used by Public Health England (PHE) in their report on e-cigarettes.
PHE published this ‘milestone’ report last month, describing it as a “comprehensive review of evidence.”
However, a number of researchers have questioned the sustainability of the data and referred to the links between some experts and the tobacco industry.
Last month, the medical journal Lancet published an editorial that attacked PHE’s ‘exceptionally shaky basis’.
Two other researchers who wrote in the BMJ have wondered whether the arguments were “built on rock or sand.”
Martin McKee, Professor of Public Health at the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine, and Simon Capewell, a professor of clinical epidemiology at the University of Liverpool, say: ‘The main principle of public health is that practices should be based on proven effectiveness.’
They say the public expects that PHE’s claim ‘according to today’s best judgement, e-cigarettes are about 95% less harmful than traditional cigarettes’ is based on a detailed assessment of the evidence.
In fact, it comes from one 12-person meeting to develop a multicritical decision analysis (MCDA) to synthesize their opinions on the inconveniences associated with various nicotine-containing products, and the results of this meeting were summarized in the study.
McKee and Capewell say that one of the sponsors of the meeting was a company called EuroSwiss Health, whose general manager is said to have previously received funding from Bristish American Tobacco for an independent investigation.
He also supported BAT's public health powers in the sustainability report, they say.
One of the meeting's 12 people announced that they had received funding from an e-cigarette manufacturer, but not the funding he reportedly previously received from the tobacco company Philip Morris International.
The selection criteria of the panel members are not disclosed, but the panel includes a number of well-knowned e-cigarette supporters, some of whom also report that they have received funding in the field.
Others who were present are not known for their expertise in tobacco control.
The meeting was also attended by PHE's Cigarette Director.
A team-produced study ‘magnifies’ that there is a lack of “undisputed evidence regarding the drawbacks of most products and most of the criteria,” McKee and Capewell write.
However, none of these connections or restrictions are mentioned in the PHE report.
McKee and Capewell state PHE’s claims that ‘for the time side, there is no evidence that e-cigarettes serve as a route to incineration in children or non-smokers’ are premature.
Kevin Fenton, PHE's Director of Health and Welfare, said: "The claims made in the BMJ have been answered in the past.
He says, “E-cigarettes are significantly less harmful than incineration.”
Half of those who burn their lives die from their addiction.
All the evidence suggests that the health risks caused by e-cigarettes are relatively small, but we need to continue to investigate the long-term effects.
PHE has a clear obligation to inform the public of what the evidence shows and what it does not look like, especially since there is so much public uncertainty about the relative dangers of tobacco.
Nearly 80 000 people die each year from smoking-related illness and smoking costs the NHS 2 billion pounds each year.
By explaining the current evidence - that is, even though e-cigarettes are not risk-free, their disadvantages are only a fraction of the disadvantages of traditional cigarettes - we are fulfilling our national responsibilities.
“The BBC should give up EastEnders,” said former Channel 4 executive
Former Channel 4 chairman Luke Johnson has said the BBC should stop broadcasting programs such as EastEnders or The Voice, and that commercial competitors would “give anything” to hits such as this soap opera set on Albert Square, for example.
Johnson said licence fees should only be used to make programs that other British broadcasters do not.
He criticizes the BBC for launching public service programmes that are likely to have a smaller audience, in difficult broadcast times against the most watched programmes of its contestants, and on channels such as BBC4.
Since BBC CEO Tony Hall said the company wanted to cooperate more with other organizations, Johnson said he tried Channel 4 for six years to build a relationship with the BBC, in vain.
“I don’t understand the idea that the BBC is producing and broadcasting programmes that would clearly suit unsubsidised competitors,” Johnson told the Communications Committee of the House of Lords as part of its research on the BBC’s future.
If the licence fee is adhered to - and I'm not sure it would be a good idea for this media or, in the long run, it should at least limit its production to programs that others don't do and produce.
The Voice will be sent at the most important broadcasting time because they want to gather as much support as possible for its future.
The idea that no one else is showing the show is a joke, it's ridiculous, it's obviously nonsense.
Johnson said: “I don’t understand why a regressive tax should fund programs that could be financed even without state subsidies.
The Voice is one such and EastEnders another, admittedly done in-house.
To put it bluntly, other channels would give you anything for it.
I don't see how it's a public service transmission.
“I don’t think it’s going to weaken the BBC at all.”
Johnson, who leaded Channel 4 between 2004 and 2010, said the BBC was guilty of hunting viewership “because of the goals of the people working there, and because they believe it’s the best way to achieve long-term support.”
His comments on the BBC's most popular series are reminiscent of comments from Tony Ball, the director of the former Sky. He used his 2003 MacTaggart lecture in Edinburgh to suggest that the BBC would sell such series as EastEnders.
Johnson said that the BBC’s public service programmes “will go to the BBC4, which often cannot measure the number of viewers, but the BBC thus fulfils its areas of responsibility and then, when defending 4 billion pounds of licence fee income, can hypocritically state that ‘of course, after all, we are doing all these unknown programs that no one watches.’
They'll put them at a place where no one's ever coming to see them.
With regard to the licence fee, Johnson told the committee: “I challenge you to look for a more regressive system in who receives the most value for it.
Of course, you all think, we all think that for 150 pounds a year you get a lot of value, but I don't know if it's true for the people for whom 150 pounds is big money, because they don't use the BBC much.
Former Sky and Channel 5 director David Elstein, who also testified to the committee on Tuesday, said the BBC should reassess its Reith mission to “informate, educate and entertain.”
He said that only a small part of the BBC's production is now classified as education - he said the figures are 1% of TV production and 0.1% of the radio - and that it should take inspiration from Australian and Canadian broadcasters and turn the task into "information, education, and entertainment."
With regard to partnership issues, Elstein said that the BBC should focus more on events such as Proms concerts, which, according to him, are only shown on television for a few times, and allied themselves with commercial corporations in such a way that all of them can be watched online.
‘Sound Genetics’ enables the control of brain cells through sound waves
The video shows you how nematodes change direction as soon as the sound impulse hits them.
Scientists have grown worms with genetically modified neural systems to be controlled by sound wave bursts.
Tiny nematodes change direction as soon as they are hit by a sound impulse that is too high for human ears to hear.
Impulses work by turning on genetically engineered movement nerve cells with diaphragm ducts that react to ultrasound waves.
Related: Researchers Read and Write Brain Activity with Light
Researchers said the worms show the functionality of a new method called sound genetics. In it, ultrasound can be used to activate certain brain, heart and muscle cells from outside the body.
Sreekanth Chalasani, a researcher at the Salk Institute in La Jolla, California, told The Guardian that the method could one day replace deep brain stimulation. It’s an invasive procedure in which electrical impulses are conducted to the human brain to treat Parkinson’s disease.
Nematodes don't normally respond to ultrasound, but Chalasani found that when they were surrounded by a liquid with microscopic bubbles, the situation changed.
He noticed that the bubbles reinforced ultrasound waves, which then pass inside the worms.
Confirmed ultrasonic waves affect TRP-4 ion channels, the structures found in the cell membranes of some worms.
Sound waves make these ion channels open and activate the cells attached to them, according to a report published in the journal Nature Communications.
In order to produce ultrasound-guided nematodes, Chalasani genetically modified the worms so that some of their movement nerve cells have TRP-4-ion channels.
When Chalasani used ultrasound for modified worms, microbubbles reinforced sound waves and transmitted them to worms, where they turn on the modified motion nerve cells.
The method has some of the same traits as optogenetics, a pioneering tool that allows scientists to turn nerve cells on and off using light impulses.
However, Chalasani says that the use of sonogenetics may have some advantages over optogenetics.
Unlike the light that needs to be sent along the optical fiber to the desired point inside the brain, low-frequency ultrasonic waves can pass through the tissue unhindered, so they can be sent to the brain from the top of the skull.
“We believe that using gene therapy and a therapeutic virus may make it possible for the affected human nerve cells to temporarily respond to the ultrasound signal in certain neurological treatments in a clinical environment,” Chalasani said.
Other applications could focus on muscle cells and insulin-producing cells, he added.
