Eight golden rules for living in a student dormitory
It's been 11 years since I moved to a student dormitory when I didn't know, and then I still believed in berets and moral relativism, but it's only now that I'm remembering the forced smiles of the people I'm annoyed - and the numerous, long complaints from others.
Much of what I've learned can be summarized in eight simple rules.
Moving into a dormitory can be anything nerve-wranking.
It’s tempting to talk: tell me where you’re coming from, did you take a gap and where, what you’re thinking about Corbyn, and why “I really don’t love Marmite because it’s really just yeast extract HAHAHA I can be found in my room”.
Take a breath.
And also help others to take a breath - ask questions and show interest.
It guides the conversation and helps you find the people you like, and not those you wouldn't want to talk your eyelashes to even if your eyelashes are on fire and they're sharing fire extinguishers.
Give people space.
People's rooms often turn into cocktail parties, venues for movie nights, and places to spend time, but they're also the places where people dress and fix their underwear.
Related: Don't even think you're starting college when you're in a relationship
The university is a tough place for introverts.
Your room should be a place to rest, but many dorms are formed from room to dorm, which is friendly, social, and causes lifelong friendships.
It could mean hell for someone who's trying to rest.
Nothing in this culture gives you permission to say “I like you and I want to be friends with you, but now you have to leave so I can gather back the energy I spent when I talked to you.”
Try to sense when a student friend is tired of socializing, and perhaps send a text message to the door instead of knocking.
When it came to knocking: knock.
A guy in my student dormitory was of the opinion that if the door wasn't locked, he'd been invited in.
He saw quite a few things.
Make friends through baking.
Since you’re starting university in the peak season of the baking trend, knockinging on people’s doors with a chocolate cupcake plate and introducing yourself seems to some extent less strange, less ‘welcome to the neighborhood, I’ve baked something for you, now I’m smiling when I ask you for personal questions.’
Serving self-made delicacies is a great way to make friends.
Your living partners feel obligated to talk, and it may be that in the future they will associate your face with a tasty surprise.
Of course, this depends on whether you can bake.
Poorly blended flour-sugar-fat blobs are likely to cause people to just pretend sleeping when you knock.
Stealing food is a common crime in student dormitories.
Understandably, stealing a chicken is stealing, but what if you just take a drop of milk into your tea?
Ask and check with the milk owner.
It's quite possible that they'll give you permission, even if they're boiling internally.
If someone steals your stuff, it's annoying, and you'll have my sympathy, but try to treat it more adultly than my dormitory friend, who mixed his milk with detergent to punish those who stole it.
He's in jail now.
You don't have to make noise so that people don't get to sleep or study.
Not only to be a decent person, but because you're like that proverbal stew blaming the pot.
The girl in my hallway asked her neighbor if they could play their drum and bass music quieter because it was three o'clock in the morning and she had an exam in the morning.
The answer was to increase the volume.
As they finally quieted down at five in the morning, the girl kindly allowed them to sleep for two hours before making their own music roar, locked their doors and left for a three-hour exam.
Wouldn't it be romantic if your future spouse happened to move into the same dorm with you?
Or in reverse, wouldn't it be incredibly awkward if you considered a dormitory partner to be your future spouse, team up, break up, and then see him every day and possibly lose all your mutual friends to him.
Of course the heart wants what it wants, but if the hot dorm partner isn't really your future spouse, you could at least try to make sense at your bottom.
In that exhilarating moment, when you notice that someone has left their keys to their unlocked room, the line between prank and bullying is thinner than it seems.
It's a good prank, it's funny, but it's reversible in no time.
You can give a useful and fun lesson on safety by hiding something – but never mess up someone's room with toothpaste and shampoo.
Lock their doors and drop the keys into a glass of water, which you then put in the freezer, but don't attach their belongings with a blue sticker to the ceiling more than twice at most.
Racist attack on the accommodation of foreign students in Kitee - The house, which was planned as a reception centre, was smudged in Salo
A racist attack was carried out on the accommodation of foreign students on Saturday morning in Kitee, North Karelia.
Contrary to what the police said earlier, this is not accommodation for underage asylum seekers, but the sleeping quarters of foreign students who have been in Finland for a long time, says Heidi Luukkainen, a teacher at Evangelical Folk High School in Kitee.
The windows and walls of the folk high school's accommodation facilities were smeared with racist signs, such as swastikas, and a metal object had been throwned through the window of one of the accommodation premises, according to the Eastern Finnish police.
According to Luukkainen, who is the teacher in charge of the social guarantee training for young people at Kansanoposti, there were approximately ten people in the building that was the target of vandalism at the time of the attack.
"It was the weekend and adult people are free to move around, so some of them may have been elsewhere," Luukkainen says.
There are people from 12 different nationalities staying at the Folk High School.
They are students who have been in Finland for some time, as well as newly arrived minor asylum seekers.
Everyone in a messed-up building has A-statuses.
Some study in adult primary schools, some are in vocational studies, and some are studying Finnish," Luukkainen says.
‘A status’ is a so-called continuous residence permit, which can be granted for four years at a time to a foreigner who has been in Finland for some time.
The Eastern Finland Police are investigating the attack as damages and violations of domestic peace.
According to Luukkainen, a press conference was held for the students on Monday, where the necessary interpreters were also present.
There's no panic here, and no one's ever wanted to go away.
We've had foreign students for more than a decade, and this is the first time that something like this happens," Luukkainen says.
According to Luukkainen, the messes have already been removed from the walls.
At least three men are suspected to have been involved and they had been travelling in a blond passenger car.
I think this is the whim of a weekend night for young people, as this topic has now been on the surface.
There's nothing more serious," Luukkainen says.
A hospital has also been smeared in Salo, where a reception centre has been planned.
The incident happened last week.
This was reported by MTV on its website on Tuesday evening.
A swastika was drawn on the wall of the hospital and written "You are not welcome".
This has been reported to the police.
Revision 15.9. at 16.02 Corrected incorrect information provided by the police about the age and status of the people who lived in the building.
The state abolishes the Y-train to Karjaa - the passengers in their jams, "absolutely idiotic"
The Y-train from Helsinki to Karjaa in Raasepori will stop running in March.
The news indignated the passengers of the train at the Helsinki Central Railway Station.
