The H and R trains running on the main line will be combined into a new R-line.
Purola's station in Järvenpää and Nuppulinna in Tuusula will be discontinued.
The Ministry of Transport and Communications and VR released information on which routes the Ministry will buy train traffic on during the next agreement period.
The contract period will change at the turn of the year, and there will be routes to be discontinued throughout the country.
The new four-year agreement to be finalised in the autumn will later be approved by the Finance Committee.
Bengtström from Siuntio believes that the state's train cutter is a short-sighted activity.
Let's make the same mistakes that have been made in the past and repeat them in a very systematic way.
And then the remorse comes.
The Y-train is also used by people other than Raasepori and Siuntians, as it is fast and overrides several stations.
Annina Pöyry from Espoo was annoyed by train to those travelling further away from the urban area.
"It's miserable for those who have to plan their journey again," he said as he climbed the Y train.
There's going to be a little relief.
There will probably be a new train connection to the coastal track, which will replace the Y-train in the Helsinki Region Transport (HSL) area, i.e. take you all the way to Kirkkonummi.
This will bring more costs to HSL, i.e. in practice the municipalities of the region and their taxpayers.
"We are now starting to explore different options and their impact on the service level and ticket prices," said HSL's CEO Suvi Rihtniemi.
It is still impossible to accurately estimate the costs.
Rihtniemi says that based on the number of passengers, HSL has passed about one million a year from the Y-train, of course the ministry much more than this.
On the main line, the R-line will miss two stations.
On the other hand, the number of train services in Ainola and Saunakallio is doubling.
A total of 28 small stations and numerous routes will cease throughout the country.
The Ministry will no longer buy train traffic from Joensuu-Nurmes, Tampere-Keuruu and Jyväskylä-Haapamäki-Seinäjoki.
Pieksämäki-Joensuu route will be shortened to Pieksämäki-Varkaus.
Train shifts will be reduced on routes Riihimäki-Lahti, Hanko-Karjaa, Kouvola-Kotka Harbour and Kajaani-Oulu.
More than half a million trips are made per year, while the total number of journeys purchased by the Ministry is about nine million.
The new agreement is intended to bring the state the annual savings of 12.3 million.
The value of the contract is approximately 27.5 million euros per year.
Sixten Korkman wrote a chapter in The Topicality of Capital: "Marx is Worth Getting to Know"
The crises of the global economy have surfaced Karl Marx (1818-1883) and his main work Capital, published between 1867 and 1894), writes Yrjö Hakanen, former chairman of the SKP, in his book The Topicality of Marx's Capital, published today.
"He'll be taken more seriously now than in a long time," Hakanen writes in his foreword.
Marx's assessments of the development, internationalisation and intrusion of the capitalist economy increasingly for all spheres of life, the concentration of capital and the growing role of money capital and imaginary capital have proved surprisingly farsighted.
The list of authors of the book based on Hakanen's Capital Seminars also includes Aalto University's professor, respected financial expert Sixten Korkman.
In Korkman writes that Marx is topical "now and forever."
Surely the former Managing Director of the Research Institute of the Finnish Economy and the Delegation of the Finnish Broadcasting Society is not a Marxist?
Oh, no, no, no, no.
Korkman says on the phone that he does not consider Marx to be a topical economic thinker, but rather an interesting social theorist.
Today, the social sciences are differentiated and specialized, but Marx was a philosopher, historian, sociologist, economist, journalist, political agitator, and literary connoisser.
"The usual notion is that the history of ideology tells about the wrong opinions of dead men," Korkman begins.
It's true Marx's prophecies went very wrong.
Although Marx understood the nature and significance of the industrial revolution, he drew conclusions from the early stages of capitalism too far, Korkman reflects.
But Korkman nevertheless sees value in Marx's radical attitude.
"What's more relevant about Marx is that he asked good questions," he says.
Marx's interests, such as the distribution of wealth, are being discussed again.
"Marx may be able to give inspiration to those who want to comprehend the big picture, the world changing as a result of the technical upheaval," Korkman analyzes.
But in what the government should decide tomorrow will not help Marx.
In his opinion, capitalism was a hopeless case that should just be stopped.
In Korkman's opinion, it is worth getting to know Marx.
"In the 70's Marx couldn't be read," Korkman says.
“Maoists and Trotskyists and other sects all hated capitalism, but even more each other.”
Today, the debate is more diverse, and you can also approach Marx without the ideological and political ballast of the previous years.
"Every social scientist should get acquainted with Marx's world of thought - as with other classics," Korkman says.
