The demonstration also stops public transport and possibly closes kindergartens and schools.
Even if the demonstration does not receive indivisible support from wage and salary earners, it can become the largest in years.
Approximately 300 demonstrations have taken place annually in Helsinki in recent years.
They're all over the place.
Traditionally, it has been quite peaceful in the demonstrations of the trade union movement in Finland - even though the operating methods have hardened in recent years.
Traditionally, a Finnish demonstration avoids the conflict, let's be law-abiding.
Protesters are trying to avoid damaging or destroying property, Siisäinen says.
However, the end of the 1990's brought new winds.
Demonstration culture has changed.
Membership of the EU unnerved farmers to throw eggs and the police were prepared to get pig shit on them.
The firefighter strike lasted for three months in 1998.
There were also spectacular strikes for pilots and air traffic controllers, for example.
Do you remember these strikes and demonstrations?
Metal and electrical strikes of the 1970's
The most significant strikes of the 1970s were the metal strike in 1971 and the power plant and electricity strike in 1977.
70 000 metalworkers took part in the metal men's strike.
The strike was interrupted by the Reconciliation Committee's proposal after the strike lasted seven weeks.
Metalworkers received a 16 per cent wage increase.
In the spring of 1977, a power strike lasted 49 days and caused power outages around the country.
Transient Thursday in 1993
On 4 November 1993, about 20 000 people gathered in front of the Parliament House, many of those who made the recession unemployed, to demonstrate for work.
Firefighters went on strike for three months in 1998
The firefighters' strike was one of the longest in the 1990's, if not the longest national strike.
Farmers opposed the EU in 1994 and 1999
The police got into egg rain when farmers protested against the EU in 1994.
