The Austrian police have asked the National Bureau of Investigation for help in determining the background of a refugee truck registered in Finland.
On Sunday, the Austrian police stopped a truck in Aisterheim near the German border carrying 42 refugees.
It is not known whether the truck was possibly on its way to Finland.
Those on the cold truck were all in good condition.
We've had an exchange of information with the Austrian police.
Investigation and the main responsibility lies at the scene in Austria, says Chief Inspector Tero Haapala of the National Bureau of Investigation.
At the end of August, other refugees who had travelled by truck in Austria were more fatal.
At that time, all 71 passengers on board apparently died suffocating.
According to the Finnish Broadcasting Company (YLE), which first announced cooperation with the Austrian police, the truck belongs to a Finnish limited liability company.
Among other things, the company engages in restaurant operations and its CEO is a man with a foreign background, about 40 years old.
Two Iraqi men have been arrested for human smuggling in Austria.
Refugee-beating captain, former drug courier and wealthy businessman - the story of five smugglers
A smuggled king of smugglers, an abusive boat driver, a style-conscious missionary, and a taxi driver who considers himself a good guy.
For example, such people engage in smuggling people into the European Union.
Criminologist Andrea Di Nicola from the University of Trento and journalist Giampaolo Musumeci have met and interviewed dozens of smugglers and their "customers", and a book they have written The Death's Travel Agency will be published in Finnish this week.
In the book, they present the tentacles of human smuggling, propose solutions and introduce smugglers from different stages of business.
HS introduces five of them.
I'll beat them away right away
Kerkenna islands on the east coast of Tunisia do not attract foreign tourists, but many Tunisians head there for the summer.
In terms of its location, the islands are also ideal for smuggling, as it is only 120 kilometres to the Italian island of Lampedusa.
For comparison: the distance from Helsinki to Tallinn is about 85 kilometers.
There, in the village of El Attaya, lives Emir, in the chain, the burning captain and the father of six children.
Kerkenna islands are famous for their shrimps, but it's more lucrative for the fisherman to transport people.
That's what Emir has done with his blue, ten-metre wooden boat hundreds of times.
If the boat fills up, it can accommodate 40 people.
For one trip, Emir earns about 8 000 euros after the expenses.
Emir has five assistants looking for him for clients all over Tunisia.
Clients arrive from many countries, but during the Arab Spring, a huge number of Tunisians wanted to escape unrest.
The situation then calmed down until the refugee crisis.
Customers arrive from the mainland to the Kerkenna Islands in small groups.
Emir accommodates them in empty houses for as long as they see fit.
Food is given in such a way that the customers barely survive.
The accommodation costs about 75 euros per night, and if the customer runs out of money, that's their problem.
We're going to the sleet at midnight with quenched lanterns.
On the first night we drive away from the islands, then stop, wait for the day and drive the next night to Lampedusa.
Sometimes the seafare is tough, and in waves 20 meters high, the passengers cry and vomit.
At the end of the destination, the human cargo has been dismantled in half an hour and Emir embarks on a return trip.
Emir wants him to be feared.
That's why he's beating his customers, and at least he's claiming to beat the scumbagers to death.
He says in the book that when the police once overwhelmed him, he threw all his passengers overboard and told them to swim to the nearest island.
"I grabbed the customers one by one and threw them overboard," Emir says in the book.
The calmest passengers come from sub-Sahara.
I heard he's gonna have to abuse his compatriots for this.
I'll immediately beat away your beliefs about them.
You have to show them immediately who's in charge here.
It's an ordinary taxi driver
Goran is an ordinary taxi driver in Bogovað, Serbia.
His taxi isn't very good - the starter engine is on its way and it's seen its best days anyway.
However, the most important thing for Goran's customers is that the taxi moves in the first place, and that it is moving towards the Hungarian border.
Bogovaða is a small village with 500 inhabitants about 60 kilometers southwest of Belgrade.
Next to the village there is a centre for asylum seekers, where mainly families are admitted.
In the woods surrounding the city centre, men travelling without their family stay in tents, lean-to shelters and abandoned cars, which has now been discovered or built by anyone.
Families living in the forest who, for one reason or another, have not filed an asylum application in Serbia.
Some people also stay in private residences, but it's a public secret.
Among the foresters, Goran finds most of his customers.
These are often exhausted - through the Balkans, the route requires a large number of border crossings and weeks of walking in difficult terrain, often in mountainous areas and in the dark.
Arriving eventually in the Bogovavaða region, at a large hub of human smuggling, people are deadly.
Goran's riders are predominantly Syrian, Afghan, Pakistani, Iraqi and Kurds.
“Syrians are the best customers,” he said in the book.
"They'll say it once and that's enough," Goran praises.
According to him, the Africans are trying to haggle.
Goran doesn't like that.
That's a sizzling silly thing all the way away.
For example, the price is 300 euros, then they lower it to 200 and eventually offer 150.
Goran emphasizes that he is a nice and kind-hearted man.
I'm not interested in money.
I'm just doing my job and helping people," he says in the book.
Goran says he works at a lower price than the others and charges only 50 euros to the Hungarian border.
The distance is three hundred kilometers, most comfortably on the highway.
Colleagues say they're asking for a triple or quadruple price.
Goran earns about 50 000 euros a year.
It's not a great sum for the smuggler, but it's quite a nice annual income in Serbia.
Serbia has decreed that taxis should not carry migrants 12 kilometres closer to the Hungarian border.
Goran doesn't care about it: he knows the roads and shortcuts and takes his rider a mile away from the border.
He then shows his customers the right direction and hustles away.
The Work of Mercy
The 35-year-old Congolese man is called the Missionary, and it's nicknamed.
He really has a Protestant church in the Ugandan capital Kampala, which is visited by a lot of believers who have escaped from the Congo, as well as a small center where Congolese child refugees are helped.
In addition to preaching, the Miscellanous Preacher runs a smuggling business where people are transferred from Congo to Europe.
He thinks he's doing the work of mercy.
It's important for a missionary to look good.
He polishes his shoes several times a day and makes sure that the elastic band of the white underwear elegantly peeks from under the low waist of the jeans.
The missionary is proud of his new Mercedes, SUV and three motorcycles.
When the Missionary doesn't preach, he organizes people from Congo to Uganda and onwards to Europe.
Initially, the customer will call one of the Missionary's five phone numbers.
He advises that it is worth arriving at the border in the morning, hiding the money nicely and sliding among the merchants passing across the border.
In order to obtain the status of an asylum seeker or a refugee, the Ugandan authorities need to be told that they are, for example, from the war zones of Northern Kivu, and that they have been forced to flee their homes for violence.
Often it's true, but if it isn't, the Missionary organizes a new identity for the customer.
The old ID will be destroyed and replaced by another.
It's helped by the Missionary's contacts with the Ugandan police.
After this, the customer is directed to the same place where even the right asylum seekers go: to the asylum procedure, to NGOs, and finally to the UN Refugee Agency (UNHCR).
The missionary knows the bureaucracy because he himself has come to Uganda as a refugee.
He invoices for each step separately: it costs about one hundred euros to submit an application to the Ugandan police, the handling of documents to a humanitarian organization about 150 euros, and the migration process, for example, to a European country of 4 500-7 000 euros.
Foil and ice cubes
Tom's a novice smuggler, an ex-drug courier.
An Irishman in his 40s is on the coast of Fraá in Calais, where his job is to load people on their way to Britain.
He is assisted by an Afghan, French taxi driver living in London and dozens of truck drivers who may not even know that they are in the human smuggling business.
At night, Tom and the taxi driver picked up their customers, usually 2-3 people, from somewhere near the center of Calais.
They're driving highway A26 and trying to avoid the police.
If the police stopped the car on the highway, Tom and the driver would go straight to jail for arranging illegal immigration.
Tom doesn't want that, he's been sitting for seven years because of drugs.
Taxi stops, Tom and his customers run across the highway to the rest stop, where the drivers sleep before continuing across the canal to the UK.
Tom chooses a suitable truck - you have to make sure that it's on its way to England and not, for example, in the Netherlands, and cut off the safety cable of the protective cover.
You have to do it quietly so the drivers don't wake up.
Sometimes the driver is involved in the plot, but then it's more expensive.
When the route is clear, Tom wraps his customers in aluminum foil, the same kind that is wrapped in the kitchen around a roast or baked potato.
It's supposed to stop the police's heat scanner from noticing the hider
Some people use a sleeping bag stuffed with ice for the same purpose.
Tom leaves his customers in the truck and leaves, hoping that he won't drive any staggers, at least not before the UK.
King of Smugglers
Muammer Küçük is one of the world's most famous smugglers.
A rich Turk has been called the master of marketing and the undisputed king of illicit invasions of the Mediterranean, who controls maritime routes from Turkey to EU countries, mainly Greece and Italy.
Küçük has been operating since the 1990's and is constantly developing new smuggling methods.
He also has a competitive advantage over other entrepreneurs: he demands the money only to arrive.
One of the new methods is a luxury scam: Turkish ports are rented yachts to take U.S. or European families on comfortable tourist cruises for a few days.
In reality, the vessels will be loaded with Syrian, Iraqis, Kurds and Afghans, which will be transported for a fee of 5 000-7 000 euros, first from Istanbul by land to some port and then by sea to Europe.
Customers are stored in human warehouses, such as old industrial halls, until it is time for them to leave.
People are stuffed in minibuses and driven somewhere along the coast.
They are told to hide among the vegetation and wait for a sign.
When it is given, you have to get on board quickly.
Passengers need to stay hidden all the way below deck.
The Coast Guard can only observe how deep the ship swam, as there are no other signs of the crowd.
Food and drink are given, but the less the passengers defecate and vomit, the better.
Küçük receives about 53 000 euros for one luxury vessel trip after expenses.
He does not take care of practical matters himself, but hires captains, recruiters, security guards, hosters and others to take care of the jobs on their behalf.
During the summer season, at least hundreds of shipments are arranged and Küçük earns millions.
He is thought to have bought cover companies in the country, a pharmaceutical company and more than a hundred apartments that he rents.
Muammer Küçük was arrested in 2011 at the same time as Turkey's Democratic Party MP and a pharmaceutical entrepreneur.
Since then, apparently, he's disappeared.
