An American Olympic runner talks about his illness and becoming a prostitute
Suzy Favor Hamilton's new book, which competed three times in the Olympics, has attracted attention in the United States.
Favor Hamilton talks about the termination of his career and becoming a prostitute.
Sports Illustrated magazine published snippets about the book on Tuesday.
Favor Hamilton ended his career at the Sydney Olympics.
He came to the races in top shape and ran the second-best time in the qualifiers.
However, in the finals, he panicked.
From the very beginning, he rushed to the top and defended his lead with a manic run.
However, 150 yards before the finish, his powers were exhausted.
He collapsed to the surface of the track, and the other rivals overtook him one after another.
I felt like an idiot and I thought I'd never run again.
However, I forced myself over the finish line, Favor Hamilton says in his book.
It was later revealed that Favor Hamilton suffered from bipolar disorder.
His brother Dan had committed suicide a year before the Sydney Games, and he himself felt tremendous pressures of success.
Favor Hamilton was a 43-year-old mother when she became a prostitute in Las Vegas.
The couple didn't have any financial problems, but he was fascinated by the idea of getting money from having sex with strange men.
She also persuaded her husband to accept what she
Favor Hamilton's rate was 450 euros per hour.
He says he spent the money on luxury.
Another explanation for Favor Hamilton's prostitute was that he longed for the kind of adrenaline spikes he'd had when competing in athletics.
Favor Hamilton later found out that he suffered from bipolar disorder.
At the end of 2012, the twin life of a former star runner came to light, when an article was published in Smoking Gun magazine.
Favor-Hamilton took part in the Olympics in Barcelona, Atlanta and Sydney.
In 2000, he was the world's number one with a time of 3:57.40, but finished last in the Sydney Olympic final.
The world champion rejoiced when the federation disciplined the Norwegian ski rooster: "Straight back"
Norway's ski king Petter Northug has crossed the boundaries of suitability in the opinion of at least one of his colleagues.
Northug and the Norwegian Ski Federation have been wringing the whole summer and autumn, like last year, about the athletes' contract.
Even though the federation has offered Northug a better deal than for other athletes, it hasn't been good enough for a multiple world champion and Olympic champion either.
As a result of the contract dispute, the Norwegian Ski Federation decided to exclude Northug from the World Cup for the coming season.
The decision was praised by two-time world champion Astrid Jacobsen.
He said he was happy that the union disciplined the ski rooster.
"Thank you for pointing the backbone," Jacobsen wrote on his Facebook profile.
"We can't allow one active athlete to be able to avoid working for the union's sponsor," he said.
The dispute between the Norwegian Ski Association and Northugu is all about money.
Northug apparently would like to be part of the national team part of the season so that he can promote his own sponsorship.
Jacobsen said he was delighted to represent the union, which will also take care of future generations, and will not give one of the athlete's solos.
At the same time, he pointed out that the union has given Northug both economic and other privileges for several years.
That's because I've been part of the system.
Jacobseb doesn't accept that one athlete has completely different rules than just because they're the best.
On Tuesday, Northug released its own bulletin in which he insisted that he would like to play sports in Norway next season in the World Cup, as well as later in the World Championships and the Olympics.
Astrid Jacobsen won a total of six World Championship medals in his career, two of them gold.
He was part of last winter's team that won relay gold in Falun.
