Helsinki's kindergartens will not operate to their full extent on Friday, the Helsinki Early Childhood Education Department announces.
Due to a demonstration by trade unions, day care may be centralised in certain day care centres, and club activities in kindergartens and playgrounds will not be organised.
Circummentaneous day-care centres and groups of children with intellectual disabilities and people with multiple disabilities will remain open.
In addition, afternoon activities for schoolchildren with intellectual disabilities and autism are organised normally.
The Early Childhood Education Department promises to announce further changes in operations tomorrow.
Media researcher: Sipilä takes a big risk
According to Sipilä, versatile channels are needed in communications, and the Prime Minister's television and radio speeches are one channel among others.
This channel of direct speech will also be needed in the future.
This will not be the last time," Sipilä told reporters in Parliament.
Sipilä did not yet want to assess how often the Prime Minister's television and radio speeches will become.
According to Sipilä, speeches may not always be conveyed on Yle's channels in the future.
If other channels sign up as a channel, then let's watch.
Tonight this channel will be used, Sipilä said.
According to Sipilä, the state of the Finnish economy is so serious that it requires television and radiospeaking to citizens.
This issue, which we are talking about now, is quite complex and requires a longer justification.
That's why we ended up in this canal," Sipilä explained.
The speech will be sent at 21.05-21.20 on both TV1 and Radio Finland.
It can also be viewed on Yle Areena.
Sipilä takes a big risk
The media researcher hopes that Sipilä's televised speech does not indicate a more general introversion of the government.
Is there an aim to take the right to define public discourse more rigorously in one's own hands and refuse the questions of public discourse? reflects Anu Koivunen, Professor at the University of Stockholm.
Reminds me of Foreign Minister (Timo) Soini's recent comment that he does not follow the media's agenda and does not allow it to define himself.
In the name of democratic debate and publicity, I hope that these issues will not be interconnected.
Koivunen understands that the commercial media is indignant about Sipilä's announcement.
It'd be weird if the media didn't react.
The communication styles of political decision-makers aren't notification issues in a democracy.
By definition, it is up to the representatives of the media to try not to adopt the agendas of politicians.
According to Koivunen, television talk is a big risk for Sipilä.
In his speech, Sipilä would have to bring something new and special to the table in order to meet the exceptionally high expectations set for the speech.
Is there more available today that we've already heard?
Or is he able to speak in a different tone, in a different way or in different words or to say something new?
Koivunen says that if there is only more of the same old, you have to wonder why such a great gesture was made and Yle gave it an exceptional time.
Yle also takes a risk in this sense, if it is not a special situation, but a routine speech among others.
There is a danger that Yle will be labelled as a medial by politicians or Parliament and the government, which is certainly not in accordance with Yle's interests or the general policy.
A clever bet from Sipilä
Sipilä's upcoming speech will also make it important what the Prime Minister does not say, the media researcher emphasises.
In the evening, you should take a good look at what Sipilä is lacking and what is not, says Heikki Kuutti, Research Coordinator at the University of Jyväskylä.
According to Kuutti, the TV-speech is Sipilä's cunning bet, because in this way he can bring out his own views without gravel noises.
In this way, the message can be communicated by the general public without a journalistic filter, as opposed to a press conference where journalists can ask critical questions.
Sipilä ignores the media and speaks directly to the people.
However, there is a risk that the media will misinterpret something about the speech, because more detailed questions cannot be asked.
According to Kuutti, it's very important how Sipilä performs on TV - does the sweat drip, tremble his hands.
He compares Sipilä to a father figure, who now tells the people what needs to be done.
If Sipilä knows how to do his thing well, it can work.
The situation is different than if the government were informing about something, because in this it is as if a person speaks directly to another person.
This (U.S. President Barack) Obama and his associates practice regularly.
That's really exceptional
Erkka Railo, a political scientist, considers the Prime Minister's speech to citizens to be very exceptional.
According to him, it hasn't happened in decades that the Prime Minister speaks directly to the people.
Exceptional time requires exceptional means, Railo says.
The researcher speculates that the Prime Minister wants to make Finns sympathetic to the extortions ahead.
On Friday, employee organisations will organise a demonstration and strikes against the government's actions.
Paavo Arhinmäki, chairman of the Left Alliance, says on Twitter that Sipilä could give his speech on the state of the nation in Parliament as the Prime Minister's announcement, in which case the speech could also be discussed.
SDP's Chairman Antti Rinne (sd.), on the other hand, announces that he is challenging Sipilä's dialogue.
I don't have time for monologues.
Soon six months from the election.
You can't hide endlessly, Rinne writes on Twitter.
