Hicks or cool ones?
Large logos return to the shirts
Trendsetters' fronts have recently been adorned with big logos.
Until a while ago, a fashion-conscious dresser avoided clear and big logos like Crocks.
Promoting a hoodie or t-shirt badge was seen as a hickey.
But times change.
Sporty has become a real megatrend of fashion, and so have old, familiar sports fashion brands such as Adidas and Nike have risen to the wave of coolness.
And as so often before, the boundary between silly and trendy is vague: if something is already a hick, it starts to become ironic and cool.
First came the norm core trend, i.e. ordinary fashion, in which the trendsetters surprised by favoring the most boring basic clothes as a way to stand out from the crowd.
Instead of a grey basic sweatshirt, we're already wearing the sweatpant shirt buried at the bottom of the closet with the manufacturer's logo in front of it.
The bigger and more traditional, the better.
In addition to the logos, Adidas' recognisable tracksuites, favourite team jerseys and New York Yankees caps have also made a return to street fashion.
Calvin Klein logos for underwear are now a cool detail.
The trend seems to be shifting from sporty brands to luxury brands as well.
For example, Versace's autumn blouse chest makes it clear what brand of clothing it is.
Are you ready to show the logos for your dress?
