There has been a leak at the Kittilä mine of the Canadian-owned mining company Agnico Eagle Finland.
The purified process water of Kultakaivos has been leaked from the so-called tailing litter basin into the ditch.
According to the company, measures have been taken to repair the leak.
The basin contains neutralized tailing sand from the foaming process and purified process water.
In general, the water is pumped either for reuse as process water or, in accordance with the permit, is passed through the surface drainage field to the environment.
It is estimated that the leak was approximately 300 cubic meters per hour when it was detected.
According to the company, the quality of leakage water meets the current permit conditions.
No other material spilled into the environment.
The leak was detected during an inspection round on Monday morning.
The leak has not been found to cause structural damage to the dam structures of the basin.
The company says it will work closely with the authorities to monitor and remedy the situation.
The mine employs about six hundred permanent employees.
Dansken Kuoppamäki: IMF warns against cutting too much
Finland's country report, published today by the International Monetary Fund (IMF), broadly follows the views of the government and the Ministry of Finance.
The organisation urged Finland to carry out comprehensive structural reforms to overcome economic problems.
According to the IMF, the reduction of overtime allowances and paid holidays would support Finland's competitiveness.
Reforms would be needed for the entire agreement system and unemployment security.
According to Pasi Kuoppamäki, chief economist at Danske Bank, structural reforms are agreed, but there are also small differences.
The IMF's assessment contains a lot of common messages to the government's actions, but on the other hand, there are also words of caution about what should not be cut too much.
These include, for example, infrastructure investments," Kuoppamäki says Taloussanomat.
According to the IMF, growth projects should also be carried out as quickly as possible in order to support weak economic growth.
The IMF anticipates 0.4 per cent of economic growth for Finland this year, which will accelerate to 0.9 per cent next year.
Danske anticipates today, in its new cyclical forecast, that growth will fall to zero this year.
The IMF is a little more optimistic this year.
They may have been based on the most recent observations.
