More and more underage asylum seekers are arriving in Finland alone
As early as February 2015, the Swedish Immigration Service acknowledged that up to hundreds of children who have entered the country are disappearing each year.
SVT.se and Dagens Nyheter, among others, have said on their website, according to the Swedish Immigration Service, hundreds of underage applicants are disappearing in the country every year.
Juha Kannelmaa, Senior Officer at the Finnish Immigration Service, explains the situation in Finland and how it has changed.
Is it the same in Finland?
How many of the applicants will disappear "underground" in a year?- A total of 156 asylum applications were filed in Finland in 2013 for unaccompanied minors.
They disappeared 9.
In 2014, 196 similar applications were made and 11 of them disappeared, Kannelmaa says.
The disappearance may be due to the fact that the applicant has already been registered in some other country applying the Dublin Regulation and may have been found to be of legal age there.
In this case, it may be more tempting for the applicant to disappear than to continue processing the matter in Finland and to be returned to the country responsible for processing the application.
Correspondingly, some missing persons can also be found later, when they apply for asylum in another Member State.
In 2014, there were a total of 3651 asylum seekers in Finland, of whom a decision was made for 134 applications because the applicant had probably moved away from Finland, so 123 adult asylum seekers disappeared over the past year, he says.
Is the number increasing?
This year, by 6 September, we have received a total of 586 asylum applications from unaccompanied minors.
There are no overall statistics on minor asylum seekers lost this year, but the number of minor applicants is therefore known and it has clearly increased, which is also likely to affect the number of missing persons, he emphasises.
What will happen to those who have been refused?
Are they also disappearing, for example, into the "dark labour market"?
This is difficult to assess from the Finnish Immigration Service, as the police are responsible for the implementation of deportation decisions, Kannelmaa emphasises.
Minister of the Interior Petteri Orpo (Coalition Party) has been concerned about the growing number of unregistered aspirants.
He's taken action on the matter.
Orpo, who discussed refugee issues at the meeting of the EU's interior ministers, spoke on Monday about practical measures to bring the flow of asylum seekers through Sweden to northern Finland.
