Psychologist advises: How to tell children about refugees
Over the past few days, many parents have had to think about how to tell a child about the situation of refugees seeking to reach Europe.
Is it worth telling about it at all?
There is no need to forcibly raise the issue, advises Ferdinand Garoff, a member of the SPR's crisis group.
It is worth protecting smaller children, for example, not letting them watch the news alone.
Young children are not always able to understand what they see, and it can revolve around their minds and get anxious, he says.
However, if a child has encountered news photos of refugees on television or heard about it elsewhere, it is worth bringing it up.
It is a good idea to have a conversation on the child's starting point and listen carefully to what he or she asks.
A child's fears can be surprising.
They aren't necessarily what a parent thinks he's afraid of.
It's not a good idea to intimidate a child in vain, even if you're feeling anxious and scared.
A smaller child closely monitors the behavior of their parents.
If the parents themselves remain calm during the conversation, the child also feels safe.
Older children will probably come across a discussion about refugees at school, with friends or online.
It is a good idea for the parents to bring up the matter with them if they have any questions for the child.
The fact is that not all people in the world are doing well.
Parents, together with their children, can think about how we feel about this in our family.
In the end, do we think that the world is a safe or unsafe place?
Parents may also have different educational perspectives - some people may want to protect their children from anxiety if possible.
On the other hand: If the family also talks about difficult issues, the child gets a model of how to deal with the difficulties that arise in life.
This could be useful for him in the future.
