That's why you won't be able to get rid of useless iPhone programs
Apple's CEO Tim Cook.
At least some of the programs that come with Apple's iPhones can be removed sometime in the future - but not quite all.
In the future, Apple may plan to remove some of its own programs from iPhones, the company's CEO Tim Cook said in an interview with Buzzfeed.
There are a number of apps on Apple's iPhone, which aren't used by relatively few people: FaceTime calls, Newsstand Kiosk, Health, Stock Exchange, Podcasts and Tips come to mind.
The latest addition to these programs is the Apple Watch, which came as a compulsion update for iPhones, although it is only necessary for Apple smartwatch owners.
According to Cook, uninstalling Apple's programs is more complicated than it seems.
Some apps are linked to something else on your iPhone.
If they are removed, it may cause problems somewhere else on the phone," Cook told Buzzfeed.
Cook didn't mention which of Apple's iPhone apps work closely together with other programs.
There are other programs that don't work like this.
So with time, I think we can develop a way to allow the user to remove them, Cook added.
According to Cook, Apple is looking into this because the company doesn't want to waste memory space on users' iPhones.
Inflation in the euro area waned to 0.1 per cent in
Consumer prices in the euro area rose only slightly in August, or 0.1 per cent year-on-year, according to Eurostat, the EU's statistical authority.
The rise in prices was driven down by lower energy prices.
In this way, Eurostat revised its 0.2 per cent estimate a couple of weeks ago slightly downwards.
In July, inflation was 0.2 per cent.
The so-called underlying inflation fell in August to 0.9 per cent from 1.0 per cent in July.
Eurostat's estimate for underlying inflation was 1.0 per cent.
Energy was 7.2 per cent cheaper in the euro area last month than one year ago.
In the EU area of the whole of 28 countries, the average inflation rate was zero in August.
In July, the increase was 0.1 per cent.
Of the euro countries, consumer prices rose the most, or 1.4 per cent, in Malta in August.
In Cyprio, inflation was the lowest when prices fell by 1.9 per cent.
In Finland, inflation was 0.2 per cent minus, according to the EU's Harmonised Price Index.
Inflation in the euro area remains persistently far from the European Central Bank's (ECB) target, which is a two per cent price rise over the medium term.
The ECB forecasts an average of 0.1% inflation for the euro area this year.
Next year, the rise will pick up to 1.1 per cent, the central bank predicts in its new inflation forecasts published at the beginning of September.
The U.S. Fed is trying to tighten monetary policy
The U.S. next recession will begin in less than a year, but more likely within three years after the country's central bank, the Fed begins to raise its interest rates for a long time.
And in the financial markets, on the other hand, the decline begins before the next recession.
The Fed has hinted that it may start raising interest rates as early as this week and at the end of the year at the latest.
Thus, the next recession can press on at its fastest by next autumn and the exchange rate rush of the financial markets before that.
Or at least that's what's going to happen if the world's largest economy and financial markets work the way they've done, and if they're reacting to extortioning the Fed's money taps like they've done before.
Such conclusions have been drawned by Deutsche Bank's London market research strategist Jim Reid, Nick Burns and Seb Barker in their extensive long-term market and economic cycle research.
The bank distributed the Long-Term Asset Return Study, which exceeded 60 pages, to its major international clients last week.
strategists who drafted a study and a report don't think they know exactly when the Fed's going to raise their interest rates or what's going to happen in the economy and the market afterwards.
On the other hand, with their cool facts and calculations in economic statistics, they present how the economy and market have behaved in threshold and after the Fed's interest rate hikes.
In addition to historical observations, they will look at which key variables in the economy and financial markets are now on the same or another model as at the time of the previous interest rate hikes - and what might result from it.
It becomes clear that both the economy and the financial markets are now in a more shaky state to withstand the rise in interest rates than once before since the 1950s.
The beginning of the recession may not a year
Leaders deciding on the Fed's monetary policy and its current tuning have hinted throughout last summer and in the early autumn that the time is beginning to be ripe for raising interest rates.
The first interest rate hike in more than nine years could come this week.
The matter will be resolved within Wednesday and Thursday, when the so-called open market committee of the Fed's Executive Board meets to decide on the matter for its two-day meeting.
The last time the Fed raised its so-called Fed funds policy rate was in June 2006.
At that time, a series of relatively regular but small interest rate withdrawals of 0.25 percentage points ended for exactly two years, with the Fed raising its interest rates from 1.25% to 5.25 per cent.
The symptoms of the financial markets began in 2007 due to difficulties with the most risky housing finance in the United States, and a year later, the financial crisis burst into a global doomsday nightmare.
A major recession occurred in 2009.
Last time, serious setbacks in the financial markets and the real economy began more slowly than have typically occurred since the previous Fed's interest rate hikes.
Correspondingly, the ordeals were tougher and harsher than at any time since the recession of the 1930's.
Deutsche's strategists have sifted through the rises and declines of the world's largest economies, as well as the transformation of financial markets since 1950 and in some places since the beginning of the 19th century.
Since the 1950's, they've been explaining the Fed's interest rate hikes and their economic and financial effects.
It has taken less than a year since the beginning of the recession from the beginning of the recession, usually less than three years and, on average, less than three and a half years.
The rise in financial market prices has ended within one year or two since the Fed's interest rate hikes began.
Since the beginning of the 1950's, the Fed has alternately raised and lowered its interest rate by a dozen cycles.
In some respects, each time has differed from others, and the narrator is relatively scarce, so it is hardly possible to deduce anything definitive about the effects of the following interest rate withdrawals.
Strategists point out that this time a lot of things are different.
And not just better.
Heels too low for too long
The decisive causes of the financial crisis, let alone the culprits, may not be undeniably identified, even through decades of research.
But the three facts about the causes that led to the crisis and increased its destructive power are somewhat undeniably clear:
1) The Fed, the ECB in the euro area and other the world's largest central banks kept their key interest rates too low for too long in the 21st century.
They patched the tracks of the techno bubble, and at the same time increased the following.
2) The amount of debt accumulated in different parts of the financial and the real economy over the decades and already too large grew even greater due to central banks' excessive monetary policies.
The biggest debt bubble in previous economic history was created.
3) Inclusively on the financial enthusiast, cash-cheaper debt financing, which was packed almost undiscontained, swewed the largest exchange rate bubbles in most areas of the financial market, from equities to real estate, bonds and commodities.
There is hardly much controversy, either, that financial markets, which have risen to the height of increasingly complex leverages, were in an unusually dangerous and unstable state when the Fed and the other central banks last pushed interest rates up.
The financial structures tuned to the leverages, let alone the valuation bubbles, lasted only as long as interest rate withdrawals had begunned and the costs of debt financing had started to rise.
The risks were realised as a financial crisis, followed by a major recession.
A bigger bubble and more debt
Deutsche's strategists don't guess what's going to happen next, but give reasons for thinking, for example by mentioning:
1) The Fed and other major central banks have now kept their monetary policy tuning even lighter even longer than before the financial crisis.
If, before the crisis, interest rates were too low for too long, they have now been even lower for even longer.
2) Indebtedness has not been crazy-cheap for a long time like it was ten years ago, but now debt money is literally cheaper than to express.
Thus, the load of debt in the financial and real economy has not eased, but now in different parts of the economy there is a much heavier debt load than before or before the financial crisis.
3) For the most part, the liquidity generated by the central banks has remained in the financial markets for the most expensive of the key types of securities and assets, then the pre-financial crisis peak or at any time.
And the strategists don't just flatten the mood with the warning signs of the financial markets, but they also point out that even the U.S. economy hasn't yet recovered from the financial crisis and the heaviest recession in decades, as has happened in the past.
The euro area economy has scarcely recovered at all.
In other words, the real economy is unusually weak, but the pressures and risks of financial markets are incomparably greater than before the financial crisis, when the Fed next begins to tighten its financial taps tighter.
Maybe Albert Edwards, a strategist at Societe General, isn't as bizarre as it suddenly seems.
For some time now, he has been coaching the bank's international investor customers to the Fed's following turnarounds:
First the Fed raises interest rates, then the financial bubbles burst, and it's time for the Fed to bust out the financial fires with even bigger QE money syringes.
Hewlett-Packard (HP), a manufacturer of computers and printers, intends to cut up to 33000 jobs.
Most of the reductions will target HP's corporate service side, the company said.
In November, HP will be distributed to Hewlett Packard Enterprise and HP Inc., which focuses on consumer PCs and printers.
HP's workforce peaked in 2011, when it had 350000 employees.
In October last year, personnel had been reduced to 302000.
The company recognises the non-recurring expenses of 2.7 billion dollars (approximately 2.4 billion euros).
Hewlett Packard Enterprise will reduce 25000-30000 employees.
Reductions on the consumer side will remain at 3300 within three years.
HP plans to move its operations to low-cost countries so that 60% of its employees would be in low-cost countries in 2018.
The company says that it has already balanced its business in such a way that none of its customers are responsible for more than ten per cent of its operating result.
Last year, its three most significant customers brought 65 per cent of the operating result.
iOS 9: how do you get a new operating system for your iPhone after Apple's update release
You can request a new operating system by going to the Settings app, clicking “Common” and then selecting “Software update”.
If the update is ready, your phone will give you the option to download and then install it.
Some people have been having problems since this installation when a “Software Update Failed” error has appeared on the screen.
To bypass this, it is recommended that users either wait for the worst congestion to pass or try to update through the iTunes program.
The phone guides the user in the final process
This may include deleting apps to get status, but Apple has reduced the ability to do this by reducing the size of the update, and also facilitated the process with a new tool that suggests uninstallable apps and then reinstalls them when the update is completed.
You should make sure that the phone is charged or near the charger during the update, so that the battery does not run out.
If the charging level is too low, Apple won't allow the update to proceed, as if the battery runs out, it could result in an update.
