Minister Berner has served on the board of the tax haven company
According to YLE, Minister of Transport and Communications Anne Berner (Centre Party) has acted as a member of the board of the tax haven company.
According to Yle, so-called tax planning is involved in the company's operations.
It is the Luxembourg-based company Saltri II LuxCo, of which Berner was appointed as a board member in December 2012.
According to the Luxembourg Business Register, Berner is still officially a member of the board.
Berner himself says he has filed a resignation in May this year.
I've checked it and it's enough that I've filed a resignation.
It is almost impossible to manage and control that the registration chains take place within the agreed schedule, Berner says in an interview with Yle.
Membership after the merger
Threads of the Swedish company Kährs are managed through a Luxembourg company.
Kährs is a parquet manufacturer that merged with the Finnish parquet manufacturer Karelia-Upofloor in 2012.
Berner served on Karelia-Upofloor's Board of Directors in 2010-2013 and, after the merger, on Kährs's Board of Directors and the Luxembourg company.
According to Yle, Kährs' holdings in Luxembourg run through a multi-stage ownership structure.
Berner tells Yle that he accepted board membership in the Luxembourg company at the request of Kährsi's second-largest owner.
This owner is Hartwall Capital.
I've been a member of Hartwall Capital's Karelia-Upofloor board.
The company was looking for a partner that was found in Sweden.
This company was owned by a private equity investor who has a company in Luxembourg.
I felt that my own task was to take care of the realisation of the capital benefits of the Finnish family-owned company.
It is important that we ensure that the returns on the capital of our Finnish companies are taxed in Finland and that the starting point was secured in this context, Berner says.
Berner tells Yle that Hartwall Capital's holdings in the company do not involve tax planning.
According to Berner, the complex ownership structure erected in a tax haven is due to the fact that Kährsi's largest private equity fund Triton already had a Luxembourg-based holding company ready for the holdings.
A reception centre maintained by the company opens in Espoo
The first reception centre for adult asylum seekers maintained by the company will open in Otaniemi, Espoo.
The reception centre is ready to have the first one hundred residents starting this afternoon.
Within a week, the number of seats can increase to a maximum of 300-400 people.
The Finnish Immigration Service will establish a reception centre at the former Rescue College's property.
The operations are maintained by Luona Oy, whose field of activity is, in particular, the provision of healthcare and medical services.
The City of Espoo, the Finnish Immigration Service and Luona Oy jointly agree on the centre's security arrangements, among other things.
