Ahtisaari in the Guardian: Westers rejected Russia's proposal to overthrew al-Assad
More than three years ago, Western countries rejected Russia's offer to persuade Syrian President Bashar al-Assad to relinquish power, President Martti Ahtisaari told the Guardian.
According to Ahtisaari, Russia's ambassador to the UN proposed a plan whereby al-Assad would have elegantly receded from power after a while.
However, the Western countries did not seize the proposal because they believed that al-Assad would fall in a few weeks anyway.
Ahtisaari discussed the crisis in Syria in 2012 with the five permanent members of the UN Security Council.
According to Ahtisaari, Russia's envoy Vitaly Churkin listed a three-point plan for him in a one-on-one discussion: the opposition was not allowed to armament, there immediately had to be a voice between the opposition and Al-Assad, and the abolition of Al-Assad had to be carried out discreetly.
I know Tshurkin.
We don't necessarily agree on all things, but we'll be able to talk openly.
I told him what I was doing there and he said sit down, Martti, and I'll tell him what to do, Ahtisaari recalls in an interview with the Guardian.
The diplomats don't comment
Ahtisaari says he conveyed Tshurkin's message to the United States, Britain and France, but nothing happened.
Churkin did not confirm the contents of the discussion to the magazine, but according to Ahtisaari, there are no two words about it.
U.N. diplomats also declined to comment on Ahtisaari's claims, but said that Al-Assad's forces had already committed several massacres at that time, and key opposition groups refused any treaties he would have taken over.
Sir John Jenkins, the former head of the Middle East branch of the British Foreign Office, is hesitant in Ahtisaari's speeches because, according to his experience, Russia was never ready to discuss the fate of Al-Assad.
Officially, Russia has supported Al-Assad throughout the war.
The weakest part of Ahtisaari's claim is that Tshurkin was speaking on Moscow's mandate.
If he had told me what Churkin had said, I would have replied that I wanted to hear the same thing from President Vladimir Putin so that I could take it seriously.
According to Jenkins, even in that case, he would have doubted that it would have been Putin's gimmick to keep the power with some Al-Assad supporter.
Refugee stream our own actions
During Ahtisaari's negotiations, about 7,500 people had died in the Syrian crisis.
According to the UN, the number of fatalities exceeded 220 000 at the beginning of this year.
More than 11 million people have been disappeared from their homes due to the war.
We should've stopped this disaster.
Refugees flowing into Europe is one of our own actions.
I don't see any choice but to take good care of these poor people.
We are now paying the bill that we caused ourselves, Ahtisaari says to the newspaper.
Asylum Seeker Arrangement Centre in Tornio
The Government's organising centre for the organisation of asylum seekers planned for Northern Finland is likely to be established in Tornio.
The centre would be established in a building of the Tornio Joint Lyceum, which is vacant in the city centre.
The Ministry of the Interior has been looking for a suitable place to better control the flow of refugees from the Swedish border.
Helsingin Sanomat reported on Tuesday that the council had already decided to present the school building as a space solution for the ministry.
According to Sauli Hyöppinen (left), Vice Chairman of the Tornio City Council, this is not true.
When the entire council was not assembled and part of the group wanted an official decision, we decided to invite a council meeting next Monday to decide on the matter, he specifies to STT.
There were 36 delegates from 43 at the discussion.
The organizing centre was opposed by 13 delegates.
Based on that, you could expect the decision to be positive, Hyöppinen says.
According to Mayor Timo Nousiainen, the city was contacted on Monday, and the matter was reacted to quickly.
An estimated 500 asylum seekers could be accommodated in the joint lyceum at a time.
An expert has audited that it could be suitable for such use.
The location is a couple of hundred meters from the border, so it would be functional in that sense as well," Nousiainen says.
Sipilä still hopes for an alternative from the labour market to blackmail
According to Prime Minister Juha Sipilä (Centre Party), halving the holiday bonuses in the public domain would only take care of part of the government's intended reduction in the price of labour.
However, according to him, the government will be happy to accept an alternative package if the labour market organisations succeed in agreeing on one.
Sipilä told journalists in Parliament that he had not been in contact with labour market organisations.
He also did not know how seriously the organisations were looking for compensatory means for the tightening of working life announced by the government.
If such an agreement arises, we will definitely welcome it with pleasure as part of the overall package.
In Sipilä's opinion, halving the holiday bonuses would serve as a means, because it would target almost all wage and salary earners, unlike, for example, the government's planned cut in Sunday supplements.
Labor market organisations could mutually agree on a cut in holiday bonuses, as they have been enshrined in collective agreements instead of law.
The Government is expected to report to Parliament on Wednesday next week on the tightening of working life as a Government communication.
According to Sipilä, the organisations' proposals should be known before.
This is not the final deadline either.
Legislative work will then start to move, i.e. the train will be on the move and it will become increasingly difficult.
It's a good time now.
It won't be a long time in such matters if there's the will," Sipilä said.
The list published by the government last week includes, among other things, converting Epiphany and Ascension Thursday into unpaid holidays without reducing annual working hours.
In addition, the wages for the first days of sickness will be cut, overtime pay will be halved, Sunday allowances will be reduced to 75 per cent and holidays will be shortened.
The reforms will be carried out as mandatory legislation past trade unions, which has attracted strong criticism.
Due to the actions, the Central Employees' Organisations will hold a large-scale demonstration on Friday, in addition to which several trade unions have declared political strikes.
Tohmajärvi council approved the expansion of Sap Hall's warehouse
Tohmajärvi council unanimously approved the expansion of Nordic Koivu Oy's hall, i.e. sap hall's warehouse.
Criticism was prompted by the fact that the project is bigger and more expensive than was originally talked about.
There is also a busy schedule.
It was also surprising that the need for space was not known a year ago, when we decided on the previous extension.
At this stage, the expansion of the warehouse by 400-450 square meters was approved for the investment programme, but the money will be decided later in connection with the interim report.
There was still talk of an investment of 150 000-200 000 euros on the agenda, but at the meeting Technical Director Erkki Ikonen said that the price would be an estimated 300 000 euros.
The investment was supported on the grounds that it will bring work and tax revenue to the locality.
There is no risk for the municipality, as the company pays the investment as rent, Vesa Väistö (ps.) explained.
A place for the Karelian language in Joensuu
The City Board eased its earlier strong position on bringing street name signs in Karelia to the centre of Joensuu.
The new decision is important and correct in terms of preserving the Karelian tradition throughout the county.
Joensuu does not declare itself the capital of the Karelian language, because there are no historical grounds for it, and the Karelian language is not a natural part of the region's identity.
The language spoken in the Joensuu region has been more part of the eastern Savonian dialects.
Even after the new decision, there will be no street name signs in the city centre.
However, the City of Joensuu is also, on a case-by-case basis, ready to use the Karelian language, for example, in information boards and the marketing of tourism.
The city recommends the same attitude to all other actors.
Even though Joensuu is officially a monolingual municipality, it is also a good idea to be open-minded about the use of the Karelian language now that multiculturalism lives and changes strongly in North Karelia as well.
The Karelian language is still the special characteristics of the province, as is the image of Joensuu as the forest capital of the whole of Finland.
Both of them can be utilized in the county's diverse marketing.
Discontinuation of train connections cuts the province's vitality
The cuts in train connections hit the county's vitality with a heavy hand.
Transport connections have a direct impact on the county's business life and competitiveness.
And it has an impact on whether it is possible to live and work here in the first place," says Kari Karjalainen, Mayor of Joensuu.
He also highlights air traffic, the continuation of which has also been under consideration on several occasions.
It is important for the whole county that it is not possible for us to reduce our chances of declining government measures, as is happening now.
We should look at the effects of the abolitions of these transport connections, for example on employment, Karjalainen emphasises.
The deterioration of transport connections poses an additional challenge for the University of Eastern Finland in terms of attractiveness.
Of course, the better the services here, such as transport connections and the housing situation, the easier it will be to get students and employees, says Jukka Mönkkönen, Rector of the University of Eastern Finland.
He points out that poor transport connections also have an impact on the research community, which is very mobile and travels a lot because of the research.
Recruitment Manager Marko Junkkarinen says that it is already difficult to market the university towards Western Finland at the moment, because transport connections are wealthy in the transverse direction.
At the moment, about half of the students at the University of Eastern Finland come from outside Eastern Finland.
On the other hand, about 10-20 per cent of them are from Uusimaa, which is partly explained by a direct train connection.
There was one gravel sound coming from Joensuu for Siun sote
Joensuu City Board's view of the Provincial Siun Sote project is seemingly unanimous, but there is also one gravel vote.
Pentti Keskisalo, a member of the City Board (sd., in the picture), proposed the renunciation of the entire project at Monday's meeting of the board, but his proposal lapsed unprofitable.
The Board of Directors approved Mayor Kari Karjalainen's decision proposal, which will be reviewed at the beginning of October before the City Council meeting, which will issue a final statement on the project at the same time as all other municipalities.
One train service from Kotka to Kouvola will end next year
Passenger train traffic from Kotka to Kouvola will continue in the future, albeit less than one pair of trains.
There will be changes to passenger train traffic between Kotka and Kouvola next March.
One pair of trains will be left out, but you can still get from Kotka to Kouvola five times a day and vice versa.
The reductions are due to government austerity measures.
The Ministry of Transport and Communications will declinate 12.3 million euros for the next four-year period.
The quietest routes will be completely discontinued.
VR prepares new schedules by the end of the year.
They will enter into force 27.3.2016.
The boys climbed to the roof, one couldn't get down, the guys squealed
The Rescue Department released the 12-year-old who climbed to the roof, who couldn't get down from the roof on his own.
The situation arose in Tornionmäki on Ketotie at half past six on Tuesday evening.
There is an old two-storey wooden house where there are no ladders that lead to the roof at all.
It didn't stop a group of boys who decided to climb to the roof of the house along the mast of radio amateurs next to it.
The rest of the boys went down the same route, but one didn't.
As a result, the others squealed from the scene and passers-by alerted the rescue department to the site to safely return to the surface of what was left on the roof with a drawstick.
FedEx's operating proficiency is twofold, lowering the outlook for the tax year 2016
FedEx reported depressing results in its last quarter, and the delivery giant cut its full-year profit forecast due to depleted demand for freight services and higher costs for ground services.
The company also said it intends to hire some 55 000 seasonal workers for the holidays.
At this time last year, FedEx announced that it was hiring 50 000 seasonal workers for the holidays.
Its stock fell about 2.5 percent at the opening auction 45 minutes before Wednesday's opening clock.
According to FedEx Corp., it expects the tax year to end next May at US$10.40-10.90, which is 20 cents lower than the previous forecast.
Analysts were expecting US$10.84, according to FactSet.
The smaller outlook, despite an average price increase of 4.9 per cent, coming into force on January 4, and despite the higher surcharges started in November for the terrestrial transportation of heavy or large packages.
The company said it expects the operating profit to continue to grow from the previous year, as they have cut costs, revenue has increased and e-commerce is growing.
Fred Smith, the chairman and CEO, said the company “performs steadily taking into account weaker-than-expected economic conditions, especially in production and global trade.”
Plans for salaries for the duration of the holiday were announced a day after a rival UPS announced that they were going to hire 90-95 000 extra-workers, about as much as it hired last year.
In the quarter ending on August 31, FedEx's profit was US$692 million, which is 6 percent more than last year.
The profit per share came at US$2.42, which falls short of the 2.44-dollar average forecast. A survey of 12 analysts was conducted by Zacks Investment Research.
Net sales rose 5 percent to 12.3 billion dollars.
In a Zacks poll, six analysts expected 12.23 billion dollars.
In recent years, FedEx has been affected by a reduction in international priority transport, which has halted the development of the turnover of its Express unit.
However, the rise of e-commerce has improved FedEx Ground's results.
In the most recent quilt, the country unit's net sales increased by 29 per cent, but operating revenues were basically the same due to the expensive costs of larger parcels and their own insurance.
Earnings of the Express unit were better due to higher basic charges, while operating profit in freight operations was reduced by higher labour costs.
In the opening auction, about an hour before the opening clock, FedEx shares fell US$3.85, or 2.5 percent, to 150.15 dollars.
On Tuesday, FedEx sealed 11 per cent down this year and 17 percent below June's 11th record.
This week, U.S. Central Bank decision-makers are sitting around a huge mahogany table for a long-awaited meeting to decide whether the time has come to raise the key interest rate for the first time in nearly a decade.
At the other end, the central bank's decorative, two-storey meeting room is a mural from the United States.
The map is a reminder that Central Bank Chairman Janet L. Yellen and her colleagues should put the economic interests of the nation above everything else.
Congress has put the central banker's shoulders into a dual responsibility: they need to maximize U.S. employment and keep prices stable.
However, after the Great Recession, the heads of the central bank have accepted that there is also a third, unwritten mandate: financial stability.
It is precisely this third requirement that could be a decisive factor in the decision to be published on Thursday, which is so strict that it cannot even be predicted. The decision on whether the U.S. economy is ready to wean from a record-breaking incentive brought by the short-term interest rates that have remained at almost zero in late 2008 .
Although most of the data shows a stabilization of the economy, the recent turmoil in the global financial markets may have made central bank officials on pre-vacuué more frightened when it comes to increasing instability. Let's be afraid of what would make a difference if the federal funds rate value were increased even by one percentage point.
‘Understandably, the labour market and inflation are the first and most important factors to be monitored,” said Lindsey M. Piegza, chief economic expert at Stifel Nicolaus & Co.
“However, I believe that if they are looking for the justification for resuscitating interest rates, the increased uncertainty in the world market will be a valid enough reason to wait,” he said.
No agreement has been reached on the central bank's decision, which also generates nervousness in the economy.
According to Bloomberg's recent survey, about half of financial experts predict that interest rates will rise this week.
Financial markets, on the other hand, are waiting for the central bank to delay the withdrawal, as according to a key indicator, the opportunity for growth is only about 30 per cent.
This refers to a decline in securities markets if the central bank raises the level, unless the decision-makers then manage to soften the blow by promising that the next increase will not take place in the next few days.
On Tuesday, the Dow Jones industrial average index rose by 228.89 points, or 1.4 percent, with a new figure of 16 599.85.
The recovery was brought about by the expectation that the central bank will keep the level unchanged this week, says John Lonski, chief financial adviser of the Moody’s Capital Markets Research Group.
“It’s surprising if the central bank raises interest rates right now,” he says.
If decision-makers raise interest rates, “they will have to put a lot of effort in their policy to support the financial markets so that they don’t panic.”
Other experts argue that an elevated level would reassure the financial markets because it would remove uncertainty about when the central bank will operate.
Just a month ago, it appeared that the central bank would be raising interest rates this month by a quarter of a percentage point, thus taking the first small step towards a sluggish tightening of the monetary situation, which would ensure the stability of the recovery in the United States.
Yellen has stated in the spring and early summer that he expects the level to rise this year, despite the fact that liberals are calling for more economic development in their complaints.
Although the creation of new jobs slowed down in August, labour market growth has been stable this year.
Last month, the unemployment rate fell to 5.1 per cent, the lowest figure in seven years and close to the level that the central bank sees as the limit for full employment.
Wage growth has shown signs of improvement.
Although inflation is still below the central bank's annual target of 2%, according to the central bank's decision-makers, the main reason for this is the temporary impact of significantly lower oil prices.
‘The economy ‘copes with higher interest rates and also needs them, taking into account the strict situation in the labour market,’ said Ian Shepherdson, chief economic expert at Pantheon Macroeconomics.
However, financial markets around the world were shaken last week as China devaluated its currency.
The Dow Jones industrial average index fell as much as 16 percent from its May record when investors feared that China would slow the economy will also slow global growth.
The market has recovered somewhat this month, and the Dow index is now nearly 10 per cent below May's peak.
For central bank decision-makers, the turmoil in the market is an additional factor in the complex equation, which indicates when interest rates should be raised.
“Without these financial market events and last week’s coverage of China’s economic weakness, decision-makers would tighten the level at this meeting,” said Stephen D. Oliner, Senior Investigator at the UCLA Ziman Center for Real Estate and a former central bank official.
Now he predicts that decision-makers will wait at least a month to get a better idea of the effects.
“Possible effects on the U.S. economy are still quite unclear, and they don’t have much information at their disposal when it comes to the consequences of what’s going to happen in China,” Oliner said.
The well-being of the international economy is not the main concern of the central bank, so in principle it should not influence decision-making.
In practice, however, the Federal Central Bank has become the world's monetary authority.
In recent years, the Central Bank's influence has been immense.
In 2013, Ben S. Bernanken, the then chairman, hinted that the central bank could ‘narrow’ the amount of more money caused upheavals in the markets around the world.
In a world where markets and economies are becoming increasingly intertwined, central bank officials are expected not only to follow the development elsewhere, but also to consult other central banks and experts to gain a better understanding of the global economy.
The central bank’s decision-making process is ‘more art than science’, says Zachary Karabell, head of global strategy at asset management company Envestnet Inc.
Jeffrey Lacker, chairman of the Federal Reserve Bank (Federal Reserve Bank) in Richmond, Virginia this month, pointed out that “the central bank has previously overreacted the movement of financial markets that appears to be separate from the fundamentals of the economy.”
The decision-making Federal Open Market Committee has called for an increase in interest rates, and Lacker, a member entitled to vote to the Committee, appeals, as an example, to the 1998-99 decisions.
The Asian economic crisis caused the central bank to cut its benchmark by 0.75 percentage points during three meetings, “despite its limited, recognisable effects on U.S. economic growth,” Lacker said.
A year later, the central bank overturned these cuts.
The central bank should prioritise economic stability only during a major crisis, as when the market collapsed in 2008, says Adam S. Pose, a former member of the Bank of England Interest Rate Determination Committee.
The latest turmoil doesn't come anywhere near its level, Posen says.
“No one wants a situation where there is a fear of raising interest rates because the markets will be annoyed,” said Posen, chairman of the Peterson Institute for International Economics.
The Federal Reserve Act still contains a dual requirement and does not mention financial stability. As long as this is the case, I don’t think they should anticipate it.
Looking for a great Thai store?
Try LAX-C in Chinatown.
Yes, in Chinatown
This store/warehouse was opened in 1997 and is owned by LAX-C Inc.
LAX-C, often referred to as Thai-Costcona, is a Thai wholesaler that also sells to individuals.
Yes, it's in Chinatown, not Thai Town.
The shop/storage is a one-stop shop that offers a dizzling selection of fresh, frozen and dried goods, popular Thai and Asian snacks, kitchen needs, furniture, as well as religious and festive decorations.
There's also a car department in the store if you need engine oil.
The store is huge, and it takes more than an hour to get around all the corridors.
If trading makes you hungry, you can eat at LAX-C BBQ Express.
The food stall inside the store (good, cheap) is close to the checkouts, where you can find a number of takeout options.
What you have in stock: Do you need something - anything - Thai?
LAX-C will probably sell it.
The products are reasonably priced, and a large part of the products are sold in wholesale packaging.
There are Asian specialties and regional Thai herbs, fruits and other products.
The meat counter is extensive, as is the fish counter. The staff behind the counter cleans and fillets the fish and, if necessary, also fries it for you if you wish.
A large part of the shop is reserved in a huge frozen section, where you can find frozen curry leaves, furry pickles and galangal, whole ducks, fish, beef and bile, pork skin, fish buns, local sausages, sandwiches and more.
Who goes to the store: Restaurant owners and chefs, but also individual families and individuals.
Shopping trolleys and forklifts are suitable for wide corridors.
Discovery: Fresh monthly duriot next to checkout.
Where it is located: The store is located in the factory district of Los Angeles, near Chinatown and downtown - a few blocks from Philippe The Original.
Parking: LAX-C has its own parking area, which it shares with other shops and restaurants.
Even though it can be busy in the store, it's usually easy to find a parking space.
Brazil's Pediatrician Association in Rio Grande advertises a healthy diet for breastfeeders
SPRS has come up with a shocking way to try to make women understand the importance of good nutrition during breastfeeding by publishing pictures of babies absorbing breasts painted with descriptions of unhealthy food.
Breast milk consists of a variety of proteins, fats, vitamins and carbohydrates that give babies all the necessary nutrients for the development of a strong immune system.
Pregnant women and new mothers need extra energy, so they are advised to increase their daily calorie intake by about 500 calories.
However, a new study suggests that increasing the number of calories is not the only dietary change that mothers should make.
A recent study led by Robert Waterland, a professor at Baylor College of Medicine in Texas, revealed that a healthy diet for pregnant women actually contributes to the genetic manifestation of their babies and protects babies from the development of tumors.
Unfortunately, an unhealthy diet was found to negatively affect the child's immune system.
