Jarryd Hayne has many options to start his NFL career
Detroit and Seattle were both quite interested since he moved to the United States, and many other teams have been askinging about him since his impressive open training day at the end of last year.
Hayne, however, chose the San Francisco 49ers team and coach Jim Tomsula, and this seems to have been a wise decision.
Some coaches wouldn't have left Hayne's bench for the rest of the game after this Australian Rugby League convert dropped the ball in his first flight kick-off, with the team defeating Minnesota 20-3 on Tuesday.
But Tomsula trusted his husband and let Hayne return the next flood-kick and brought him into the running game after Reggie Bush's injury.
Tomsula's true nature was also reflected in the dressing room after the game.
Rather than calling his new newcomers a pathole in front of the rest of the team, he encouraged them.
Tomsula also publicly supported Hayne at a post-game press conference.
Cam Inman, a special reporter for the 49ers, reported that Hayne was also encouraged by Thomas McGaughey, the coordinator of the special teams, who advised him to forget about the dropped ball and worry about the next game.
Jarryd Hayne dropped a floor-kick that the Minnesota Vikings snatched in the first quarter of the game
This was certainly not the beginning that Hayne and his numerous Australian fans wished for his NFL debut.
Haynen was seen cursing on the way from the field after the fall, and later she told Australian radio sled Hamish & Andy 2DayFM on frequency 10.1 that she couldn't have imagined a worse start to her NFL career.
“It’s just one of those things, I just have to think about the next game and that’s it,” Hayne said.
Hayne compared his groping to his 2007 Origin debut when an assist in his own goal area led Queensland.
All in all, it was still a great experience for this 27-year-old.
After the game, Hayne posed in a photo posted on her Instagram account with fans, holdinging an Australian flag.
Elton John and Russian President Vladimir Putin will meet to discuss the rights of gays
In 2003, Russia's richest man, Mikhail Khodorkovsky, was arrested with a gun, threatening a Siberian runway.
Khodorkovsky had openly questioned President Vladimir Putin, so he was condemned, his oil company Yukos was seized and his efforts to support democracy were limited.
4 were killed when police fired rubber bullets against protesters in Nepal
Four people, including a four-year-old son, were killed in southern Nepal when police fired rubber bullets into a crowd that protested against the country's new constitutional proposal.
The violence took place in the Rupandeh region about 175 miles west of the capital Katmandu.
Bishnu Prasad Dhakal, the region's senior official, said more than 1000 protesters had gathered outside the police station, and some of them were throwing stones and firebombs.
Dhakal said the police used tear gas and then fired into the air before resorting to rubber bullets.
Twenty police officers and five civilians were injured, he said.
It wasn't immediately clear why rubber bullets, known to have been killed, but traditionally used as a less dangerous option, seemed to have caused so many deaths.
The clash was the last of weeks of violent protests across Nepal's southern plains, where members of an ethnic group called Madhesi say that the new provinces established under the proposed new constitution undermine their political influence.
There have also been protests in Western Nepal when the Tharus group calls for its own state.
Since 10 August, at least 40 people have been killed in violent classhes, 11 of whom were police officers.
At that time, the four main parties signed an agreement to divide the country into provinces - this is a big step towards a new constitution, but a very controversial issue in Nepal, which has had a central government for centuries.
A new constitution has been planned since 2008, but legislators accelerated the process after the devastating earthquake in April killed thousands of people.
Legislators have voted this week on constitutional draft clauses in Katmandu, and authorities have said that the new Constitution could be adopted by Sunday.
The U.S. National Hurricane Center (NHC) says there's a 60 percent chance of a whirlwine south of Cape Verde
On a wide low-pressure front located about 560 km south from the southernmost part of the Cape Verde group of islands, there is a 60% chance of developing into a tropical whirlwind in the next 48 hours, the U.S. National Hurricane Center said on Tuesday.
On the second attentive low-pressure frame, located halfway between the Cape Verde island and the Small Antells, there is also a 60% chance of developing into a tropical whirlwind over the next two days.
Google launches donation campaign to raise US$11 million for refugees
Google Inc (GOOGL.O) launched a donation campaign aimed at raising US$11 million for humanitarian organizations to help thousands of refugees who have come to Europe as they escape from war-torn and impoverished countries.
Google had an exceptionally personal point of view when it published the donation campaign on its blog.
Rather than having been notified by a manager, Google employee Rita Masoud, who fled Kabul at the age of seven.
‘Our journey included a number of undeclared train and bus trips, as well as hunger, thirst, coldness and darkness,” he wrote.
Well, I was lucky.
But as the refugee and migration crisis in Europe has widened, people like my family desperately want to help.
The donations will be received by four non-profit organisations that help refugees and immigrants: Doctors Without Borders, the International Rescue Committee, Save the Children, and the United National Commissioner for Refugees.
According to Google, it responds with the same amount to every donation made worldwide on google.com/refugeerelief up to US$5.5 million, with a total target of US$11 million.
Europe is experiencing the worst refugee crisis since World War II, mainly due to Syria's four-year civil war, which has driven more than 4 million people out of their homes this year.
Many are also fleeing war-torn Afghanistan, Yemen, Iraq and Libya.
Reporting Yasmeen Abutaleb, editing Christian Plumb
Weight loss surgery can help mildly obese diabetics
According to a new study, it may take at least five years for mildly obese type 2 diabetics to benefit from weight loss surgery.
However, it's still too early to say whether these mildly obese diabetics will live longer after weight loss surgery than the ones that are treated in other ways than surgery.
‘It’s going to take a long time to get mortality information,’ said Dr. Robin Blackstone. He’s a slimming surgery specialist who wrote an editorial for a new study in JAMA Surgery.
Different methods are used to reduce the size of the abdominal gaut for weight-losing or bariatric surgeries.
They reduce the feeling of hunger and limit the absorption of food.
Over the years, bariatric surgery has been proven to be effective in the treatment of type 2 diabetes, but the majority of the subjects who participated in the study have been morbidly obese, and their BMI (BMI) has been 35 or more.
BMI, i.e. the ratio of weight to height, is considered normal when it is between 18.5 and 24.9.
If the BMI is more than 25, it indicates that someone is overweight, and if a person's BMI is more than 30, they are considered to be obese.
You can calculate your BMI here: 1.usa.gov/1D0ZqDv
For this new study, researchers at Min-Sheng General Hospital in Taiwan used data collected since 2007 to compare two types of bariatric surgery, gastric bypass surgery, as well as gastric bypass reduction, for medical treatments for type 2 diabetes in people who were slightly obese.
The study showed that the average BMI of people undergoing surgery fell from 31 to 24.5 by the end of the fifth year.
At the same time, BMI remained roughly the same for people who were treated by means other than surgery.
36% of the people undergoing surgery had a complete improvement in diabetes and 28 per cent partially.
By comparison, in the medicinally treated group, diabetes was completely cured by only 1% and partly by about 2 per cent.
In addition, blood pressure, triglycerides and the ‘bad’ LDL cholestor ‘were generally better in the surgery group’, the authors noted.
Researchers also monitored patients' blood hemoglobin A1c levels, which reflect blood sugar levels in the long term.
Hemoglobin A1c levels should remain below 7%, the researchers wrote.
The post-surgery haemoglobin A1c level dropped from about 9% to about 6 percent.
However, in the medical care group, it remained stable at around 8%.
However, this better ‘glycemic control’ described by improved haemoglobin A1c levels did not reduce mortality over a five-year period, according to principal investigator Dr Chih-Cheng Hsu and his colleagues.
The research team also compared the average results between two different types of surgery.
At the fifth-year period, when compared with a group that underwent gastric reduction surgery, the gastric bypass group had lost more weight (18.7 and 14.2 kg), lowered their body mass index (7.4 and 5.1), lowered their haemoglobin A1c levels more (3.1 per cent and 2.1 percent), and was more likely to have achieved complete diabetes remission (46.9 per cent and 16.7%).
According to Blackstone of the College of Medicine-Phoenixin at the University of Arizona, even though the study doesn't show survival or surgery after five years, there are research results from Sweden, where the surgery led to a longer life in heavier people.
The Swedish study found that obesites who underwent weight losing had a 29 percent lower chance of dying over a 15-year period when they were compared with a group that had experimented with more traditional treatments.
Although the new study does not show an improvement in the survival of surgical patients, Blackstone says the results are nevertheless encouraging for people with a BMI of less than 35 who are interested in surgery.
“Study results are crucial precisely because they show that surgery is safe for a lower body mass index group,” and it doesn’t involve an increased risk of death or kidney disease, he says.
Blackstone adds that both patients and their physicians should take weight very seriously when the patient's BMI is between 27 and 30.
“I think we’ll wait too long before we take this seriously,” he said.
I believe that once a person has collected these genetic changes that permanently prepare their body for obesity and diabetes, it is very difficult to reverse the situation.
‘Not everyone can come to Europe’ – Dalai Lama - RT News
The Dalai Lama has said that Europe cannot accept all refugees, adding that a real solution to the current refugee crisis can be found in the Middle East.
The leader of Tibetan Buddhism has called for an end to the violence in refugees' home countries.
Dalai Lama discussed the ongoing refugee crisis in her speech at the opening ceremony of the Dalai Lama Centre for Compassion in Oxford at the beginning of its ten-day visit to the UK, dedicated to ethics research.
The Dalai Lama argued that, in the current situation, the needs of all mankind should be put ahead of individual peoples or even continents.
He also stressed that, although the response of Western European countries to the refugee crisis is admirable, it is also inadequate.
‘Not everyone can come to Europe,’ he said.
Although the Dalai Lama praised Germany's and Austria's actions in the crisis, he stressed that this was only a temporary solution.
‘It’s great to take care of several thousand refugees, but in the meantime it’s necessary to think about long-term solutions: how real peace and real development can be brought to these Muslim countries, mainly through education.
The most important thing is to think about how we can reduce killing in their countries.
We need to reduce the use of force.
The use of force has never solved these problems,” he added.
Taking a stand on the issue of violence, the Dalai Lama also commented on George Bush's actions after the 9/11 terrorist attacks, claiming that the U.S. Violent response caused drastic events in the chain.
“After the 9/11-Attack, in a letter, I expressed my condolences to President Bush and told him that a solution to this problem can be found through nonviolence.
I know his motives were good, but he used force and it created unexpected consequences,” Dalai Lama said.
Over the past four years, swallowing children's hand disinfectant has increased fourfold
Poisoning data centers across the United States have reported an increase of nearly 400 per cent in swallowing an alcohol-containing hand disinfectant for children under the age of 12-year-olds since 2010, according to Georgia's Poisoning Information Centre.
According to Gaylord Lopez, director of the Georgia Poisoning Information Centre, the cases of swallowing disinfectant reported at the Poisoning Information Centres for children under the age of 12 rose from 3266 cases in 2010 to 16117 cases in 2014.
“Children are more interested in these products and, unfortunately, a certain percentage of them are put on duty,” Lopez told CNN.
He said that some children drink disinfectant on purpose, while some want to impress their friends or meet the challenge on social media.
There are videos on YouTube where teenagers drink disinfectant to get a cheap boom.
According to reports, teenagers have mixed disinfectant into an alcoholic mouthwater to get a stronger drink.
Pleasant scents of disinfectant can attract younger children.
‘The child doesn’t think it’s bad for him,’ Lopez said.
Many of the most interesting (hand sanitizers) are ones with the added smell.
There are strawberry, grape and orange-scented disinfectants that are very appealing to children.
Hand sanitizer can contain alcohol between 45 and 95 per cent.
Especially in young children, only a few injections are required for alcohol poisoning.
“It’s really condensed alcohol,” Mr. Stephen Thornton, chief medical director of the University of Kansas Hospital’s Poisoning Information Center, told Fox 4 in Kansas City.
So you wouldn't leave a whiskey hot to lie on the table, but hand disinfectant is left visible, and if children are interested in it, it's a quick way to drink a lot of alcohol.
Nhaijah Russell is a six-year-old girl who some time ago swallowed only four squirts of strawberry-scented hand sanitizer at school and was taken to an emergency room for treatment.
His blood alcohol level was .179, which is twice the limit for drunk driving for adults, according to first aid outpatient doctor Chris Ritchey, who treated the girl at Gwinnet Medical Center outside Atlanta.
Nhaijah mossed as she spoke and couldn't walk when she arrived at the emergency room.
Doctors followed him overnight in a separate children's hospital for possible brain injury symptoms, as alcohol caused the girl to fall and hit her head, Ritchey said.
“It was really scary,” Nhaijah’s mother, Ortoria Scott, told CNN.
It could've been deadly for my child.
Lopez has recommended that parents and teachers use non-alcoholic products or sanitizing wipes and move the disinfectant away from children.
In addition to alcohol intoxications, some disinfectants have been associated with deaths.
In 2013, two Ontario women died after swallowing hand sanitizer, which contained a toxic, unannounced ingredient.
The health authorities assume that the product contained methanol, a lethal substance, rather than ethyl alcohol, which had been reported as an active ingredient.
In January, in the northern state of New York State, three fourth graders planned to poison their ‘evile’ teachers by spreading a bacterial toxin into the classroom, according to a police report.
The teacher is very allergic to hand sanitizer and forbids it from his class.
The police consider the plan to be prevented as ‘idle nonsense’ and leave disciplinary measures to the school district.
