Why do I testify for execution in Oklahoma
In the absence of any legal intervention, later today I will watch when the man is executed.
If that happens, and I hope not, I'll sit in the gallery of spectators at Oklahoma State Penitentiary to watch Richard Glossip be executed with a lethal injection.
He's asked me to be one of the four official witnesses of his death.
I received a request in January through his best friend, and although I was shocked, I agreed.
I've begun to dread this day as it approaches.
So why did I agree?
I agreed because I thought it'd be a good way to tell the whole story of the death penalty.
It would provide a unique perspective.
Of course, journalists have the opportunity to follow executions in America, and since there are often more applications than vacancies, participants are selected through an abominable draw.
Oklahoma has subdued seats reserved for the media between twelve and five.
Whoever you choose, you'll look at the event when it's cool.
Well, I can't do it.
I'm going to sit next to Richard Glossip's closest friends, and I've also created calls made to him over the months, as well as a two-and-a-half-hour visit.
I like a man.
We often laugh when we talk.
Prison officials say I can't be both a witness and a reporter, so I can't have a notebook and a pen with me to record what I've seen.
I just have to try to remember all the details.
A reporter from Oklahoma accused on Twitter that I had compromised my neutrality.
Here's the lesson.
Tell the prisoner's story as he or she likes, and you'll be able to participate.
Complained to the reporter.
He admitted that ‘could have set his words better,’ but he still thinks ‘what I’m doing is wrong.
Richard Glossip has disagreed with some of the things I've said and what I've written.
