Hewlett-Packard cuts up to 30 000 jobs
Hewlett-Packard is prepared to cut between 25 000 and 30 000 jobs as part of its restructuring and cost-cutting efforts in the business services business.
Later this year, HP will split into two exchange-traded companies, separatining its computer and printer businesses from fast-growing enterprise equipment and service operations.
Expected job cuts will bring costs of approximately US$2.7 billion, starting from the fourth-quarter, HP said in a statement.
“These restructuring measures will allow for a more competitive and sustainable cost structure for the new Hewlett Packard Enterprise,” said Meg Whitman, HP’s chairman and CEO, who will lead the unit after division.
“Hewlett Packard Enterprise is going to be smaller and more focused than current HP, and we’re going to have a broad and deep range of services to help companies move into a new kind of business,” Whitman said.
As a separate company, our ability to meet the evolving needs of our customers around the world is better than ever.
Hewlett Packard Enterprise’s annual revenue will be more than US$50 billion, and it “focuses on producing unmatched integrated technology solutions,” the company said in a statement.
