“Sometimes when recovering from an injury can be a pronount season when you have to strengthen your bodie and gain competition,” Wenger said.
Jack's young enough to get over it.
I hope that his body will stabilize, I'm sure it's going to happen, and that he's going to have a career like he deserves.
Wenger is preparing to recycle their squad when Arsenal start their Champions League game on Wednesday night against Dinamo Zagreb, thereafter against Chelsea on Saturday and Tottenham Hotspur next Wednesday.
Héctor Bellerín and Aaron Ramsey have not travelled to Croatia, which means that Mathieu Debuchy and Arteta are likely to get started.
Wenger can also replace Kieran Gibbs and Olivier Giroud with Nacho Monreal and Theo Walcott.
‘I’m tryinging to maintain balance and give a respite to the players who need it,’ Wenger said.
Although Arsenal are the clear succession favourite in Group F, Maksimir Stadium in Zagreb is one of Europe's scariest.
Dinamo also hasn’t lost in 41 games since they’ve produced their “Undefeated” campaign last season.
The stadium was rocked by an Earthquake on Tuesday night, a magnitude of 3.2 on the Richter scale.
It was the 10th in Zagreb this year.
Wenger believes Arsenal have learned from last season's Champions League surrender against Monaco, and he is still motivated by a distant dream to lead the team to its first European Cup victory.
“This motivation is huge,” Wenger said.
Arsenal have never achieved it.
We were very close in 2006, but on the other hand I can be realistic.
We're ambitious, but we'ren't dreamers.
FriendsFest: a comedy series that taught us serious things about friendship between men
It's striking how similar descriptions don't exist in the series, especially given that men's need to open up is more despised than ever, and the suicide rates of young men are higher than in fifteen.
In 2013, suicide was the leading cause of death among men between the ages of 20 and 45 in the UK and is three times more common in men than in women.
Campaign Against Living Miserably (CALM) was announced for the year of the 2015 men's year. The goal was to challenge a culture that prevents men from seeking help when they need it.
Friendship is at the heart of facing this challenge.
More than 2000 secondary and upper secondary school pupils were involved in a recent study that showed depressive symptoms. According to the study, a person’s chance of recovering doubled if a high-enough percentage of their friends were “in a healthy mood”.
Similarly, those who were not depressed were halved if they had mentally healthy friends.
But in order for a friendship to work, it has to be available first.
In this period of communication through social media, the style goes beyond the content and the immediacy of the answer is often more important than meaningful contact.
Our digital lives have doubled the need to appear strong, cheerful and successful, when in reality isolation dominates.
This superficial success is dominated by the values that psychologists call external: money, imago, social status. All this is done at the expense of internal values – the personal growth and friendship of internal longing – that have a deeper impact on our mental health.
And as the billionaire video game developer recently explained, money really doesn't get greater happiness.
The beauty of Frends was the fact that it turned this focus on its head.
Not a single character defined their work, but rather their personalities, which was clearly distinguished when interacting as friends.
Joey and Chandler's numerous hugs, moments in comfortable chairs looking at football, Ross longing for Rachel - their humour came from the knowledge that yes, men can relate to all of this, even though they often hesitate to fully explore their feelings.
“Listening to people who are depressed and suicidal, I’ve really understood how even the slightest interaction with a friend can make you feel better.”
Men who contact us often feel that they cannot talk to friends.
The old idea of ‘getting over’ is strong, as is the feeling that if problems unfold, they will be seen as weak.
Frendit is one of those series that comes to mind when I'm trying to offer longer-term means of survival.
It emphasized the small moments of friendship between men.
Although my impartial, external position on the other end of the phone helps people with moments of despair, long-term support comes from a close, personal network of family and friends.
And if you're taking part in Comedy Central FriendsFest this weekend, and all the locations have been redesigned to celebrate, why don't you take your friends for coffee in Central Perk.
Or, what's even better if you see someone solitary at Starbucks, smile and say hello.
You never know how much your friendship could mean.
Frends will be broadcast at Comedy Central from start to finish, all 236 episodes, take part in #FriendsFest
Di Maria scores and helps PSG slap Malmø aside
Angel Di Maria and Blaise Matuid and Edinson Cavan are celebrating goals against Malmö.
Angel Di Maria scored in their Champions League debut, helping PSG to overtake the Swedish team Malmá on Tuesday.
Below the match report and reactions to the game.
Angel Di Maria and Edinson Cavani were on the right line when Paris Saint-Germain started their Champions League game with a nice 2-0 win against Malmø at the Parc des Princes on Tuesday.
Di Maria scored less than four minutes in his Champions League debut, pointing to the French league gamesters, but the team missed several chances before eventually stepping down as Cavan striked a goal in the 61st minute.
Paris, who is in Group A with Real Madrid and Shakhtar Donetski, needed a strong start against the Swedish league champions, as there was not only a follow-up place in the game, but also an improvement in their quarter-final-leading games in the last three rounds
But even though PSG took the points, it wasn't a classic game, as Laurent Blanc's Zlatan Ibrahimovic missed a spot in front of the goal and was later replaced on the field. Malmö is his old hometown team and the team he started his brilliant career in 1999.
“It’s not easy to win in the Champions League at home or away, so a 2-0 win creates problems for the opponent and looks good in the stats, and it’s a good start, even if there are things we’ll have to hone this season,” Blanc said.
This anticipates the good.
We wanted to win and won, even though I'm sorry we couldn't score one or two more goals.
Ibrahimovic returned to the Parisian team after one of the three changes scored after a 2-2 draw in Ligue 1's home game against Bordeaux at the weekend, but was overshadowed by Di Maria in the fourth minute.
As he transferred from Manchester United last month, the Argentine winger was named a man taking PSG to the next level in Europe, and he proved why, as Marco Verratt's pass run behind Malmö's defensive line and then hit a beautiful finish past Johan Wiland into the back corner of the net.
However, if someone was expecting an easy and big win from PSG, they were disappointed.
Malmö was a congested opponent. They beat Celtic in the decision-match when they reached this level, have nine international players and a five-man defensive and two players in a deep midfield.
But the team didn't offer much on the offensive side other than Nikola Djurdjic's shot in the 34th minute, which narrowly went past Kevin Trapp's goal post.
PSG, on the other hand, sometimes got into trouble due to missed passes and a poor finish, especially from Ibrahimovic, who failed in a total of five attempts in the first half alone and then was disappointed when Cavani put the ball in contact with Ibrahimovic right at the start of the second half.
However, he was much more effective when doing preliminary work for his colleagues, and it was from Ibrahimovic's pass that Di Maria sent a spiral kick that tilted around Wiland's goal post at the 52nd minute.
The second goal was finally scored after an hour of an hour's game, thanks to Cavan, the Uruguayan who scored his sixth goal of the season, after Ibrahimovic had snuggled Maxwell's cruciate pass from the left.
That's where Malmö's hopes of getting back into the game, and only Wiland's brilliant save from David Luiz's straight kick kept the final numbers down before Ezequiel Lavezzi's goal was disqualified at the end of the game.
Malmö coach Age Hareide later admitted that PSG were at the same level as his team. The Norwegian said: “Our most recent Champions League game was in December last year.”
Since then, we've only played in the Swedish league that's not at the same level.
Qualifying matches aren't enough.
I'll take my hat to Paris.
This was a great game for them.
Glencore raises US$2.5 billion by selling shares
The Switzerland-based mining giant Glencore, which was suffering from a commodity price crash, raised US$2.5 billion on Wednesday by selling shares as part of its extensive debt-cutting plan.
Glencore, listed on the London Stock Exchange, said in a statement that it had sold new shares worth about 1.6 billion pounds to repay its debt.
A company that lost 57% of its market value this year is struggling with the prices of collapsed basic commodities as the slowdown of the Chinese economy puts a strain on demand and wreaks havoc in all markets.
The new entry raised the value of Glencore's shares to the top of the London Stock Exchange in Wednesday's morning trades.
Shares rose 2.77 per cent to 131.60 pence in the FTSE 100 index, which opened 0.69 per cent higher.
Glencore had unveiled a US$2.5-billion-dollar stock trade the previous week as part of its broader plan to cut its US$30 billion debt by about a third.
The company sold the new share capital by 125 pence shares, which meant a 2.4 per cent reduction at Tuesday's closing price.
The company sold 1.3 billion shares with a total value of 9.99 per cent of the group.
Concerns about the long-standing stagnation of China's growth have cut iron ore prices by about half, and at the same time the prices of coal, copper and other commodities have fallen by 20-40 per cent.
The White House confirms Xi Jinping's visit to Washington
“China President Xi Jinping will make a state visit to the United States at the end of the month,” the White House said.
Xi's first official visit to Washington takes place on September 25 and is a response to President Barack Obama's visit to Beijing last November.
U.S. President and Co-Speaker Michelle Obama will hold a dinner for Xi and his wife, Peng Liyuan, after the leaders hold bilateral talks.
“The visit of President Xi will provide an opportunity to expand cooperation between the United States and China on numerous global, regional and bilateral issues of common interest, and at the same time will give President Obama and President X the opportunity to constructively discuss issues with disagreements,” the statement added.
During a visit that was first announced in February, Xin is also expected to deviate in Seattle on his way to Washington to meet with a number of U.S. technology leaders before continuing towards New York City and the U.N. General Assembly in September.
Obama and Xi are expected to discuss a number of controversial issues, including Washington's concern about China's alleged cyber espionage and the country's increased unconditionality in controversial waters of the South Chinese Sea.
Matt Damon downplays diversity in filmmaking
Matt Damon clashed with African-American filmmaker Effie Brown as they talked about a difference that Damon says isn't needed behind the scenes.
In an episode of HBO's Project Greenlight on Sunday night, Brown was interrupted by an actor who emphasized the need for different perspectives when it comes to the black protagonist of the script selected for the project.
‘When we talk about diversity, it is taken into account when selecting actors for the film, not the series,’ Damon argued.
Shocked Brown reacted incredulously, commenting ‘Oh! Wow. Okay,’ while The Martian’s actor’s comments were left hanging in the air.
Other producers Ben Affleck and the Farrelly brothers joined Damon and Brown to discuss the finalists of the series.
“I’d like to urge people to think, whoever the director is, how they’re treating Harmony’s character,” Brown said.
The only dark figure is the whore that his white pimp hits.
The Dear White People’s producer demanded the rights to direct the film to the finalists Leo Angelos and Kristen Brancaccio; Damon harshly replied that their directing “could end up giving us something we don’t want.”
Once the conversation had been broadcasted, social media exploded Damon's comments.
‘Matt Damon talking to the only dark-skinned person in the room and explaining his difference is SO WHITE that it hurts.’
After its release, the tweet collected more than 5000 retweets and 4000 likes.
Damon's comments coincide with an accurate racial presentation in the media in the forefact of social discourse.
After a white police officer killed a teenage Michael Brown in 2014, The Black Lives Matter moved nationwide and urged legislators and authorities to put an end practices that discriminate discriminated against people in color.
In February, singer Prince referred to the movement when presenting the Album of the Year nominees for the Grammy Award.
‘Albums still matter,’ he said.
Like books and black lives, albums still make a difference.
Black Lives Matter's executives recently released a statement to answer Fox News television face Elisabeth Hasselbeck's question about whether the group should be considered a hate group.
“The Black Lives Matter Network is a love group,” the leaders said.
It was recently reported that Damon is involved among entertainers, producers and media moguls who have been summoned to visit the pope to discuss the image of the Catholic Church in the West.
It's unclear whether he's accepted the invitation or has already met the pope.
First American Ninja Warrior champion crowned
On Monday, professional climber and tableware collector Isaac Caldiero became the first athlete to win the American Ninja Warrior race and was rewarded with a million-dollar grand prize.
There has never been a rival in the competition program that ended its seventh season, which would have survived all the sections of the programme's obstacle course.
In the final episode of the series, two men, Caldiero and cameraman Geoff Britten, made it to the final round.
In the last mission, you had to climb a 23-metre rope in 30 seconds.
Britten climbed first and cleared the challenge on time, making him the first athlete of all time to unravel American Ninja Warrior's exhausting 4th leg. However, Caldiero cleared the challenge three seconds faster, making him the winner.
Caldiero gave an interview to E!News after an awkward victory and was still in shock to win the million-dollar grand prize.
“So it’s great to think about this, since I’ve never earned or lived more than 10 000 dollars a year,” Isaac told E! after a big raise.
So all of a sudden...so I can't even imagine what it's going to be like...the future is unknown.
Production director Kent Weed sat down to discuss with The Hollywood Reporter in the final and why Britten doesn't get more remuneration money, even though he's the first rival of all time to have managed to pass the track.
According to the rules, the money goes to the fastest person.
‘If more than one person passes, you’ll get the money for the one with the fastest time,’ Weed said.
Even though money is a great reward and transforms your life, he still gets a great sense of satisfaction.
I don't think he's feeling a grudge because of it.
He's just so happy he's done what he did.
His fans have praised him.
He's an amazing guy, and he's a good family man.
He's the hero of his children and his wife.
Geoff said to me, “If anyone had to beat me, I’m happy it was Isaac.”
And I believe that Isaac would have thought the same if this had gone the other way.
American Ninja Warrior will return for the eighth season, filming will begin in the spring of 2016.
